WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:08.670
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:12.240
It is an absolute pleasure
and delight and honor for me

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.590
to invite you all to this
evening's wonderful lecture

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:19.190
as part of our Distinguished
Speaker Series.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:24.190
And our speaker today is
Professor Susan Hockfield

00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:29.390
whom many of you from the
Yale community might remember.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:34.390
And she will be talking
to us about her new book

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.260
titled "The Age of Living Machines:

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.560
How Biology Will Build the
Next Technology Revolution".

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:46.560
Before I turn over to our dean
of faculty, Tamar Gendler,

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.020
to give a more formal
introduction to Susan,

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.450
I just wanted to share a few things.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.910
First, I wanted to give
some bookkeeping notices

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.640
that I request you all to mute your audio

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.970
and your video because we
are recording the proceedings

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:04.810
and you will have an opportunity
to interact with Susan

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.200
and ask questions through the chat

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.780
and we'll keep the chat open

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.950
and we'll read out your questions to Susan

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.590
at the end of her talk.

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.910
So I also want, at this point,

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.210
to thank Richard and Barbara
Franke who are joining us

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:21.220
from warm and balmy Arizona

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.760
for their generosity and support

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.560
of the Franke Program
and Yale more, generally,

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.320
which allows us to do lots of innovative

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.970
across disciplinary programming.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:36.690
And I think at this point,
the only thing I want to say

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:41.690
is that I have a personal
fond recollection of Susan

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.410
because by the time I joined
Yale as a junior faculty,

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:48.750
she was the provost and
it was just wonderful

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:52.910
to have someone warm and understanding

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:55.270
and absolutely brilliant and supportive

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:57.000
to be in the leadership.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.890
So Tamar, I would like to
turn it over to you now

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.633
to formally introduce Susan.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:03.303
- Thank you so much.

00:02:03.303 --> 00:02:06.510
Thank you, Priya, thank you to the Frankes

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:07.660
who are here with us today.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.481
Welcome, President Salovey

00:02:09.481 --> 00:02:14.420
who is also present at
today's Franke Lecture.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:15.270
I'm Tamar Gendler,

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.940
I'm dean of the Faculty of
Arts and Sciences here at Yale

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.320
and it is hard to think of somebody

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.800
who epitomizes the values
of the Franke Program

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.940
more completely than Susan Hockfield.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.400
The Franke Program is
devoted to bringing together

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.200
multiple perspectives, in particular,

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.510
the humanities and the sciences

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:39.090
to think about the most profound questions

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.550
that the world brings to us.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:44.250
And Susan Hockfield has made a career,

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.920
both intellectually and pragmatically

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.445
in her leadership as a
scholar and in her leadership

00:02:51.445 --> 00:02:55.210
as an academic university citizen

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:58.460
of bringing together
things that other people

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.116
didn't notice the connections.

00:03:01.116 --> 00:03:04.550
In some ways, this is no surprise.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:07.640
After earning a BA in biology

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.740
from the University of Rochester

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:14.290
and a PhD from Georgetown
School of Medicine,

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:18.190
Susan Hockfield joined Yale in 1985

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:21.950
after a post at the Cold
Spring Harbor Laboratory

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:23.490
of James Watson.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.980
Yes, that James Watson.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.800
The Watson of Crick and Watson.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.290
And this experience of having been present

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:36.310
at moments of origination,
present at moments of discovery,

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:40.840
present at moments
where unexpected insight

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:45.840
led to genuine change is
characteristic of her time

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.990
as an academic and as a university leader.

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:53.260
She joined Yale, as I said, in 1985.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.320
She was tenured in 1991,

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:58.590
the same year I note that she was married

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.060
in our very own Battell Chapel

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:05.000
and became a full
professor three years later

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.493
in the Department of Neurobiology.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:12.800
As a scholar, she excelled but perhaps,

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.360
even more striking was her capacity

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.340
for university leadership.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.050
She was appointed dean of
Yale's Graduate School in 1998

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:25.110
and stayed in that role
for four years till 2002

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:28.490
when she took on the role as Yale provost.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:32.750
And sadly, for us, but
happily for academia,

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:35.360
served in that role for two short years

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.170
before she was taken by our neighbor

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:41.840
to the North Massachusetts
Institute of Technology

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:43.880
where she served as a visionary

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:48.880
and transformative president
of MIT from 2004 to 2012.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:53.510
As I've said already
what is characteristic

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:57.310
and distinctive and exciting
about Susan Hockfield's work

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:01.710
is the way in which it involves
bringing things together.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:04.965
People, organizations, ideas.

00:05:04.965 --> 00:05:09.773
At MIT, she had the
idea that one could use

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:13.930
as a locus of organization,

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:18.210
the convergence of engineering
and the life sciences

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:21.700
and through that insight,
brought the world things

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:24.030
like the David Koch Institute

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:27.732
For Integrative Cancer Research
and the Ragon Institute

00:05:27.732 --> 00:05:31.090
of the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology,

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:34.160
Harvard University and
Mass General Hospital.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:36.780
That is she broke down the boundaries

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.980
between the biological and
the engineering sciences.

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:41.330
She broke down the boundaries

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.670
between institutions in Cambridge.

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:45.260
She broke down the boundaries

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:48.470
between the private sector
and the public sector.

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:51.290
For example, catalyzing the development

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:54.830
of the Massachusetts Green High
Performance Computing Center

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:58.540
in Holyoke, which brought
together five universities,

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:02.640
two private companies and the
Commonwealth of Massachusetts.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.800
Exemplary for our own
ambitions in planetary science

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.660
are the ways that she developed at MIT

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:13.250
on multidisciplinary institute-wide center

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:18.250
around energy which brought
the MIT Energy Initiative

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:20.910
to bring together individuals

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:24.900
from almost every academic
department at the university

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:27.920
to think about technologies and policies

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.200
for a sustainable energy future.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.510
That practical ability
to bring things together

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:38.460
is combined with an intellectual ability

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:41.720
to bring things together,
which all of us are here

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:44.897
to hear evidence of and her new book,

00:06:44.897 --> 00:06:46.450
"The Age of Living Machines",

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:48.940
which serves as a beautiful
background for her

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.740
and should serve as a beautiful background

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:54.786
for your own bedside
table in the coming weeks

00:06:54.786 --> 00:06:57.240
is an extraordinary exploration

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:01.240
of how biology will build the
next technology revolution.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:02.980
And with that introduction,

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:06.330
I suspect you have no
longer any interest at all

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:07.840
in hearing from anyone

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.130
but the extraordinary Susan Hockfield.

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:11.300
Welcome home.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:13.163
We are thrilled to have you.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.020
- Thank you so much, Priya and Tamar

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:20.507
for both the invitation and
both of your overly generous

00:07:20.507 --> 00:07:23.163
and insightful introductions.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:28.160
As Priya has just mentioned,
I have a long history at Yale

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:31.102
and immense fondness for
this great university

00:07:31.102 --> 00:07:32.633
and for its people.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:36.800
It is an indescribable privilege

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:39.490
to join one of Yale's great conversations

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:42.894
here in the Franke Program
in Science and Technology.

00:07:42.894 --> 00:07:47.160
I offered very special thanks
and very special fondness

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.070
to Rich and Barbara Franke.

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:52.980
Great to see you who have
done so much for Yale

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.340
and who played such important roles

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:57.550
when Rick Levin recruited
me to his leadership team

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:00.745
as dean of the Yale Graduate
School of Arts and Sciences.

00:08:00.745 --> 00:08:02.960
And, of course, you
all know that that team

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:05.170
came to include President Peter Salovey

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:07.830
and Rick's leadership team is well-known

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:10.380
for producing leaders, not just for Yale,

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:11.740
but for other institutions

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:15.930
and MIT did take advantage
of it in recruiting me.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:19.750
But I have to say, I can
never thank Rick sufficiently

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:22.370
for changing the direction of my career.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.620
I was on a path to understanding

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:26.170
how the brain assembles itself

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:28.603
over the course of human development.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.370
He recruited me into a new
path that he had confidence

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:35.370
I would find as interesting
and as challenging

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:39.560
and that was to understand
how people can assemble

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:43.550
to accomplish more than anyone
can accomplish on their own,

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:47.193
which has been a great
challenge and great joy for me.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:51.360
And for me, the thesis
of this lecture series

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:56.030
is central to how I think and
work as Tamar has described

00:08:56.030 --> 00:08:59.458
and this is the
cross-disciplinary conversations

00:08:59.458 --> 00:09:04.450
catalyze new insights, they
amplify ideas and their impact

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:07.350
and they're, just frankly, a
lot of fun to participate in.

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:13.700
That thesis of facilitating
cross-disciplinary conversations

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:17.360
is really the furniture
of the mind, if you will,

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:21.060
that was fostered during
my 20 years at Yale.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:23.180
So with that short preamble,

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:24.850
I'm gonna start sharing my slides

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:27.310
so we can get started on today's topic.

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:29.020
Now, I have to say I'm gonna do this

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:32.490
with some little bit of
technological challenge but.

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:37.490
So, following the theme of
cross-disciplinary conversations,

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:40.600
today, I'm gonna share some thoughts

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.290
on our shared possible future.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:45.940
I'm gonna talk about
today part of the story

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:48.970
that I've captured for general audiences.

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:50.600
This is important in my book.

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:52.240
The book is not for a
scientist or engineer,

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:55.610
it's for just plain old
people who wanna understand

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:57.810
what the future holds for us.

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:01.970
What is our very probable
technology future?

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:06.010
So, I, obviously, have
had the amazing privilege

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:08.530
of leading one of the
world's great universities

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:10.200
and as MIT's president,

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:12.761
MIT is just a little
bit of an unusual place,

00:10:12.761 --> 00:10:14.910
but I had a breathtaking perspective

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:17.660
of the science and technology frontier.

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:20.180
It's a frontier of ideas, of discoveries,

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:22.890
of applications and much, much more.

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:26.060
On the future, I saw was,
frankly, astonishing.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:27.670
And today, I'm gonna
share with you a glimpse

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:31.670
of that very probable technology future.

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:33.180
And I wanna say at the outset

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:35.595
that while I'm optimistic
about that future,

00:10:35.595 --> 00:10:36.810
those of you who know me

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:39.690
knows that I tend to
be crazily optimistic,

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:43.110
I would say at this outset,
we have to all agree

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:46.090
that the future looks a bit bleak.

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:48.470
So today, I'm gonna share both my optimism

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:49.340
and also my caution

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:52.340
because, I think, both of them
really do need our attention.

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:54.720
So just to give you a sense

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.810
of some of the daunting
challenges that we face,

00:10:57.810 --> 00:10:59.930
the planet's current population

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:04.600
of about 7 1/2 billion
people is projected to grow

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:08.170
to about 10 billion people by 2050.

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:11.433
And the demands of those 20 billion people

00:11:11.433 --> 00:11:14.303
will be unprecedented.

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:17.880
And let me just call
out just a few of them.

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:19.320
We think about healthcare.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:22.510
I mentioned here access,
accuracy and cost.

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:27.360
We spend, in the United
States, about 18% of GDP

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:30.890
on our healthcare provisions.

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:32.860
And while we do some amazing things,

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:35.780
frankly, we don't get good enough results

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:38.030
from that 18% of GDP.

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:40.630
We need to do better in many dimensions.

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:43.450
We need to be able to provide
high quality healthcare

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:45.693
to the poor and the rich alike.

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:49.550
Energy is a hugely challenging problem.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:52.293
We already do not produce

00:11:52.293 --> 00:11:56.420
the energy we need and use sustainably.

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:58.520
And it's pretty addicted that by 2050,

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:01.830
there will be a doubling of demand.

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:05.140
We simply have to figure out
how to provide the energy

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:07.170
that the world's population needs

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:09.133
without ruining the planet.

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:13.410
In terms of water and
food, we don't, today,

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:15.900
produce enough of either clean water

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:19.003
or nutritious and healthy food

00:12:19.003 --> 00:12:22.490
that is enough for our current population.

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:23.923
And think of this,

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:27.090
that if we were simply to use

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:30.200
today's agricultural technologies,

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:35.200
to feed 10 billion people what
require additional farmland

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:40.490
equal to the landmass of
South America and Africa.

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:43.130
Clearly, that is not gonna happen.

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:44.423
So we need to develop technologies

00:12:44.423 --> 00:12:48.060
that are gonna produce
the food and the water

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:50.510
that 10 billion people will need.

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:54.480
Now, of course, if we
don't meet the demands

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:58.490
of 10 billion people in
these ways and others,

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:00.173
we can anticipate chaos.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:04.210
And this problem of a growing populations

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:05.840
rapidly increasing demand

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:09.023
on the planet's sources is not new.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:12.010
We have heard the story before.

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:14.940
Many of you know so of the
Reverend Thomas Robert Malthus

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:20.360
who in 1798, wrote a fascinating treatise.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:23.940
He called it "An Essay on
the Principle of Population".

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:25.840
He did a brilliant demographic study.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:28.260
He was a great demographer.

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:29.770
And he looked at Britain and Europe

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:32.980
and he made the observation
that at that time,

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:34.330
at the end of the 18th century,

00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:39.180
the rate of population growth was faster

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:43.460
than the rate of increase in
agricultural productivity.

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:46.850
And I can tell you, don't
need to know any higher math

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:51.000
to understand that this is
a recipe for catastrophe.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:53.900
Malthus did a demographic
study over history

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:55.560
and he showed that there had been cycles

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:57.980
of population rise and fall.

00:13:57.980 --> 00:14:02.980
And the periods of population
rise always ended in tears.

00:14:03.140 --> 00:14:05.813
War, famine, pestilence.

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:10.300
And this problem of rising population

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:12.350
faster than rising resources

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:16.400
has come to be known as
a Malthusian dilemma.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:20.260
So, Malthus in 1798 was
sounding a warning cry.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:23.480
Watch out, the end is
near, we're about to enter

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:25.810
another one of these catastrophic periods.

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:28.150
But guess what?

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:30.950
At that time, he was wrong.

00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:34.200
There was not catastrophe over the horizon

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:36.810
and he was wrong because new technologies

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:39.103
averted the predicted disaster.

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:42.010
What were the new technologies?

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:47.010
Among them were the application
of four-field crop rotation

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:49.270
by farmers in Britain, thereby,

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:52.040
increasing the productivity
of their crops.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:54.450
And a marvelous new technology

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:56.320
that was one of the
most important products

00:14:56.320 --> 00:15:00.270
of those seafaring explorers
who were going across the seas

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:01.430
around the world.

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:03.520
And while, when I read about them

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:05.350
when I was in fourth grade,

00:15:05.350 --> 00:15:07.890
they were in search of gold and tobacco

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:11.170
and spices and jewels and
all kinds of exciting things.

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:12.820
But actually, they came upon something

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:15.670
that was even more important in commerce

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:19.220
and that was islands that
were inhabited only by birds

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:22.430
and were essentially huge reservoirs,

00:15:22.430 --> 00:15:26.880
enormous piles of guano, bird poo.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:30.310
Well, guano is an excellent fertilizer

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:33.950
and they're developed a very
robust trade in fertilizer,

00:15:33.950 --> 00:15:36.840
I will call it for this
group to be nice about it,

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:40.010
around the world that really accelerated

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:42.020
the rate of crop productivity.

00:15:42.020 --> 00:15:45.744
And contrary to Malthus's prediction,

00:15:45.744 --> 00:15:47.460
people in Britain didn't starve.

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:50.760
They became well-fed and
actually, population grew.

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:54.510
And what I often reflect
on is it was just in time

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:56.930
for the workforce that was needed

00:15:56.930 --> 00:15:59.230
for the industrial revolution.

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:01.260
Now, today, we are facing, once again,

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:02.510
a Malthusian dilemma.

00:16:02.510 --> 00:16:04.320
And today, I would offer the possibility

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:07.530
of using this same kind of solution.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:12.103
We need to invent our way out but can we?

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:15.420
Can we defeat Malthus again?

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:18.140
How do we chart a course
to a better future?

00:16:18.140 --> 00:16:20.590
Now, I didn't appreciate this, of course,

00:16:20.590 --> 00:16:22.180
until I joined the Rick Levin team

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:24.250
but one of the central responsibilities

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:26.360
of every university president

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:29.470
is to imagine what will be
needed to solve the problems,

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:33.350
not of today, but of the distant future.

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:34.750
And I don't know about all of you

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:38.143
but my crystal ball gets pretty
fuzzy about five years out

00:16:38.143 --> 00:16:40.970
and I needed to come up
with a way to think about

00:16:40.970 --> 00:16:43.310
what that future might possibly be

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:46.980
that didn't require this
lousy crystal ball that I had.

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:51.330
So, the strategy to imagining
the future that I adopted

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:54.600
was I thought if I could
understand the future

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:57.310
we're living in today and how we got here,

00:16:57.310 --> 00:17:00.200
perhaps, that would give
me a path to understanding

00:17:01.037 --> 00:17:03.950
what the future we could might anticipate

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:05.580
being living in tomorrow.

00:17:05.580 --> 00:17:08.320
So, let's just start by what is the future

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:09.493
we're living in today.

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:13.122
Does anyone have one of
these in this audience?

00:17:13.122 --> 00:17:15.003
Of course, we all do.

00:17:15.003 --> 00:17:17.873
These amazing products,

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:21.400
these digital products, these cell phones

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:25.040
that every one of us carries
around in our purse or pocket

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:28.170
represent the major technology
story of the 20th century.

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:30.600
It is the Digital Revolution.

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:33.560
I'm gonna divide my observations
today into three parts.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:36.230
First, I'm gonna talk about how did we get

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:39.670
to the digitally enabled
world that we live in today.

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:41.130
I'm gonna make the case

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:44.600
that digital technologies
today are a product

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:48.240
of the convergence of
physics with engineering,

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:50.120
which I'll convergence 1.0.

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:53.760
I'm gonna explain the
history of the digital world

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:57.853
and say history of discovery
to marketplace technologies.

00:17:58.810 --> 00:17:59.960
And then the second part,

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:02.790
I'm gonna offer a view of
tomorrow's likely future.

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:05.080
Tomorrow's transformational technologies

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:06.790
and where they're gonna come from.

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:07.950
And just the other preview,

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:10.930
we are on the cusp of a new convergence.

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:15.040
Convergence 2.0 of
biology with engineering.

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:16.490
It's already in progress

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:18.610
and it's rapidly accelerating

00:18:18.610 --> 00:18:22.730
and to my mind, it promises technologies

00:18:22.730 --> 00:18:26.400
as impactful and as transformational

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:29.003
as well the digital
technologies we enjoy today.

00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:31.470
And lastly, the third part,

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:32.630
I'm gonna close with some thoughts

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:35.860
on what we need to do to
accelerate those technologies

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:38.130
and the bottom line is, as I said before,

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:42.040
to defeat Malthus again,
we need to innovate.

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:45.550
So where did the Digital
Revolution come from?

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:46.540
The Digital Revolution

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:50.040
is a product of 19th century physics.

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:53.307
Curiosity-driven, fundamental discovery

00:18:53.307 --> 00:18:57.430
and fundamental discovery, we
know well in the university

00:18:57.430 --> 00:19:02.430
is investigations without any
practical application in mind.

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:04.460
So let me just let's go
over a bit of history

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:06.680
that's likely familiar to many of you.

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:10.670
On the left, I'm showing a
drawing of Michael Faraday.

00:19:10.670 --> 00:19:13.480
Michael Faraday was
probably one of the world's

00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:17.540
greatest investigators of
the behavior of physics.

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:20.910
He studied electromagnetism
and other physical forces

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:23.320
and as I said, he was
studying the behavior

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:25.360
of these physical properties,

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:29.490
not the nature of electricity
or electromagnetism.

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:31.230
He didn't know what the forces were

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:32.950
that were giving rise to these,

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:35.290
the properties he was studying.

00:19:35.290 --> 00:19:39.750
And it wasn't until J.J.
Thompson shown on the right,

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:42.150
discovered it'd be electron in 1897

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:43.610
that we began to have an idea

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:47.450
of what those forces were
behind Faraday's observations.

00:19:47.450 --> 00:19:50.980
And it wasn't, of course,
Faraday alone or Thompson alone,

00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:52.710
there was a whole group of people

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:56.210
who were busy just had to understand

00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:58.760
how these things worked.

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:02.330
With the elucidation the
electron, the proton, the neutron,

00:20:02.330 --> 00:20:04.890
X-rays and other forces,

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:09.890
we got into our hands, a parts
list of the physical world.

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:16.070
Electrons are a fantastically useful part

00:20:16.070 --> 00:20:19.780
and one of the important parts
of the physical parts list,

00:20:19.780 --> 00:20:21.930
physical world's parts list.

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:24.440
Of course, my dear friends, the engineers

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:26.220
like nothing better than a parts list

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:30.280
and engineers picked up
those physical world parts

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:34.730
and turn them into practical applications.

00:20:34.730 --> 00:20:37.300
The electronics industry was born

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:38.610
and in that and industry,

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:41.100
we got vacuum tubes and transistors,

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:44.650
which enabled any number of
new kinds of technologies.

00:20:44.650 --> 00:20:47.010
Broadcast radio and television,

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:49.340
long distance telephony

00:20:49.340 --> 00:20:53.423
and a whole universe of new technologies.

00:20:54.420 --> 00:20:57.350
And then the electronics industry

00:20:57.350 --> 00:21:00.420
has had some very useful progeny.

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:03.500
the computer and information industries.

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:05.170
This is the story of discovery

00:21:05.170 --> 00:21:07.480
leading to industrial products,

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:11.390
which have fueled
economic and jobs growth.

00:21:11.390 --> 00:21:12.960
It has been very productive,

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:15.810
not just in terms of these
digital technologies used today

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:18.630
but in terms of bettering
the lives of many.

00:21:18.630 --> 00:21:20.790
Now, the electronics
industry was coming along

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:25.320
at a reasonable pace but
frankly, a little sleepily

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:27.373
and invariably,

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:32.390
technology's most powerful catalyst

00:21:32.390 --> 00:21:35.363
is unfortunately, war.

00:21:36.409 --> 00:21:40.670
Napoleon recognized this when in 1805,

00:21:40.670 --> 00:21:44.970
he repurposed Ecole Polytechnique
into a military academy.

00:21:44.970 --> 00:21:46.640
He understood that to wage the wars,

00:21:46.640 --> 00:21:50.430
he was waging and about
to wage successfully,

00:21:50.430 --> 00:21:53.640
he needed to have the best
technology on the planet

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:55.500
and so did we.

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:58.080
So during World War II, the United States

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:02.400
and all the other nations
invested massively in technology.

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:04.340
It's a fantastic story and of course,

00:22:04.340 --> 00:22:07.310
I can't dig into enormous detail

00:22:07.310 --> 00:22:12.310
but our nation's massive World
War II investments in R&amp;D

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:17.120
led to technology miracles
and ultimately, won the war.

00:22:17.120 --> 00:22:18.870
Now, there's a whole list of technologies

00:22:18.870 --> 00:22:21.580
that came out of World War
II would include radar.

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:25.620
Many consider radar to be
the war-winning technology.

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:28.873
Sonar, the atomic bomb,

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:32.190
certainly, the war-ending technology.

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:34.870
But in addition, the foundations
of computing relayed,

00:22:34.870 --> 00:22:37.410
the foundations of GPS and the Internet,

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:39.750
we're all part of this
incredible technology

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:41.870
and push for World War II.

00:22:41.870 --> 00:22:45.020
The man who led this
effort was Vannevar Bush

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:46.740
who had been dean of engineering at MIT

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:48.383
and then president of Carnegie.

00:22:49.370 --> 00:22:53.823
So it was he who organized
this incredible effort.

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:58.110
Now, as the war was drawing to a close,

00:22:58.110 --> 00:23:02.440
President Roosevelt, FDR, turned to Bush

00:23:03.380 --> 00:23:07.823
and asked Bush to prepare a post-war plan.

00:23:09.133 --> 00:23:12.510
Vannevar did so and the plan,

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:17.390
which was published in a fantastic,

00:23:17.390 --> 00:23:18.740
just amazing treatise

00:23:18.740 --> 00:23:20.877
called "Science, the Endless Frontier".

00:23:22.670 --> 00:23:25.530
Put forward the thesis, that briefly,

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:27.010
that the lessons learned

00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:29.070
in the wartime application of science

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:32.020
can be profitably applied in peace.

00:23:32.020 --> 00:23:35.490
This was incredibly different

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:40.410
and startlingly counterintuitive.

00:23:40.410 --> 00:23:44.660
After prosecuting a war
successfully, a nation is bankrupt.

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:47.420
They've been drowning all these debt.

00:23:47.420 --> 00:23:52.210
And the biggest force after
a war is to reduce the debt

00:23:52.210 --> 00:23:54.140
and pull back on expenditures

00:23:54.140 --> 00:23:57.240
and Bush was advocating
exactly the opposite.

00:23:57.240 --> 00:24:01.320
Bush advocated that rather than pull back,

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:04.880
we should double down
on those investments.

00:24:04.880 --> 00:24:06.530
In "Science, Endless Frontier"

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:08.430
he lays out all the different ways

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:10.890
these investments should be placed.

00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:13.660
A powerful blueprint for
America's second half

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:15.000
of the 20th century.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:18.620
It was a blueprint for
economic, industrial

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:21.410
and sociological expansion.

00:24:21.410 --> 00:24:24.220
He advocated for federal investments,

00:24:24.220 --> 00:24:27.450
not just in research but in education.

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:29.730
You all know about the GI Bill

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:32.907
which provided education for returning GIs

00:24:32.907 --> 00:24:35.070
who many of whom had come off the farm.

00:24:35.070 --> 00:24:37.490
But to come back and get an education,

00:24:37.490 --> 00:24:39.570
college or community colleges,

00:24:39.570 --> 00:24:43.020
there was money for
mortgages and much more.

00:24:43.020 --> 00:24:46.400
He described a plan to
accomplish these efforts.

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:49.710
Essentially, he laid out all of the levers

00:24:49.710 --> 00:24:53.493
necessary to drive technological
and economic progress.

00:24:54.430 --> 00:24:57.270
And his plan would set the foundation

00:24:57.270 --> 00:25:00.510
of the 20th century's most
transformational technologies

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:03.770
and industries including
the computer and information

00:25:03.770 --> 00:25:06.160
and aerospace, biomedical industries

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:08.660
and everything that the
United States produced

00:25:08.660 --> 00:25:10.610
in the second half of the 20th century.

00:25:11.570 --> 00:25:15.950
However, his supporter, FDR died.

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:19.560
And FDR his successors
were not as enthusiastic

00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:22.490
as building further debt.

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:27.490
And so, they were following
Bush's plan, I would say,

00:25:29.112 --> 00:25:33.460
a little bit more slowly
than Bush or FDR would have.

00:25:33.460 --> 00:25:38.200
So the post-war effort
got off to a slow start

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:41.123
until Sputnik.

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:46.910
1957, the Russians beat the United States

00:25:46.910 --> 00:25:49.633
into space with Sputnik.

00:25:50.780 --> 00:25:53.190
And I recall this as it's
though were yesterday,

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:56.580
that blinking light of Sputnik
traveling across the sky

00:25:56.580 --> 00:26:00.740
cast a very dark shadow
across this nation.

00:26:00.740 --> 00:26:03.990
And in response, President Kennedy

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:05.743
announced a race to the moon.

00:26:06.630 --> 00:26:09.770
And Kennedy gave any
number of speeches about

00:26:09.770 --> 00:26:11.940
that quite inspirational
and this is a quotation

00:26:11.940 --> 00:26:14.770
from a speech he gave at Rice University.

00:26:14.770 --> 00:26:17.520
And I wanna move this in a
direction beyond the technology.

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:20.570
He says, "We choose to go
to the moon in this decade

00:26:20.570 --> 00:26:23.770
and do the other things,
not because they're easy,

00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:25.530
but because they're hard

00:26:25.530 --> 00:26:28.240
and because that goal
will serve to organize

00:26:28.240 --> 00:26:32.137
and measure the best of
our energies and skills."

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:38.300
And this shared ambition,
this national goal

00:26:39.730 --> 00:26:43.948
that Kennedy called out
drove both national security

00:26:43.948 --> 00:26:47.700
and economic and jobs growth.

00:26:47.700 --> 00:26:51.010
It was a far-reaching
catalyst for technology,

00:26:51.010 --> 00:26:53.543
for education and for industry.

00:26:54.750 --> 00:26:56.440
A certain national ambition reaches

00:26:56.440 --> 00:27:00.730
well-beyond those directly
engaged in the effort.

00:27:00.730 --> 00:27:03.513
I wanna offer one example.

00:27:04.530 --> 00:27:09.530
Now, people often ask me
how I got into science

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:13.433
and how I got from science
to the presidency of MIT.

00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:17.150
Well, the simple answer is that I grew up

00:27:17.150 --> 00:27:18.603
under the shadow of Sputnik.

00:27:19.710 --> 00:27:22.780
This is me with my three sisters

00:27:22.780 --> 00:27:25.830
and I was very fortunate,
we were all very fortunate

00:27:25.830 --> 00:27:30.120
in having parents who basically told us

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:33.450
that we could do anything that we wanted.

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:35.320
We just had to have the ambition

00:27:35.320 --> 00:27:37.473
and the willingness to work hard.

00:27:38.530 --> 00:27:43.530
So for me then, and for my generation,

00:27:43.990 --> 00:27:47.460
growing up under the shadow
spot wasn't the shadow of fear,

00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:50.770
it was the bright beacon of inspiration.

00:27:50.770 --> 00:27:52.650
What science could bring the world

00:27:53.570 --> 00:27:56.290
without any boundaries in
terms of what I could study

00:27:56.290 --> 00:27:59.340
whether it was science
or history or English.

00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:04.150
I heard President Kennedy's
call to action and I echoed it

00:28:04.150 --> 00:28:07.190
by articulating the call for
science in the new economy

00:28:07.190 --> 00:28:08.400
and found my calling.

00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:10.190
Now, this next slide is
a little embarrassing

00:28:10.190 --> 00:28:14.970
but I had to include it
to demonstrate what power

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:19.970
a national ambition has
even on second grade kids.

00:28:20.170 --> 00:28:23.194
So this is an essay, an illustrated essay.

00:28:23.194 --> 00:28:25.533
It's a challenge to call it illustrated

00:28:25.533 --> 00:28:28.130
because I wasn't a good illustrator

00:28:28.130 --> 00:28:29.910
and it's a little embarrassing
to share with you.

00:28:29.910 --> 00:28:34.120
I wasn't that good of drawing
or you can see, at spelling

00:28:34.120 --> 00:28:39.120
or at grammar or penmanship
and I would admit readily

00:28:39.290 --> 00:28:42.260
that all those deficits remained with me.

00:28:42.260 --> 00:28:44.470
However, even as a second grader,

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:48.370
I got caught up in the enthusiasm
of this national ambition.

00:28:48.370 --> 00:28:50.930
That Sputnik, that bright beacon through,

00:28:50.930 --> 00:28:53.670
not just me, but a whole
generation of children,

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:57.250
fueled our national and soon
to be international ambition.

00:28:57.250 --> 00:29:00.350
So whether you're
directly involved or not,

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:02.410
you experience a sense of reflected glory

00:29:02.410 --> 00:29:06.400
and a sense of everyone
working toward an ambition

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:07.963
that's greater than your own.

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:11.180
So, the other thing I
wanna call out from this

00:29:11.180 --> 00:29:12.600
is the last sentence.

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:13.940
I don't know how I knew this.

00:29:13.940 --> 00:29:18.010
I was in second grade that
you could figure things out

00:29:18.010 --> 00:29:19.770
maybe figure out how to go to the moon

00:29:19.770 --> 00:29:21.980
but it would be fun.

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:25.680
So I recognized even then the magnificent

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:30.680
and exciting paradox that you
can pursue your personal dream

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:32.740
and serve others.

00:29:32.740 --> 00:29:35.860
And it is, in many ways,
a description of the power

00:29:35.860 --> 00:29:37.690
of shared ambition.

00:29:37.690 --> 00:29:40.150
So, the Great War and
then great shared ambition

00:29:40.150 --> 00:29:43.130
and Cold War fueled the most
powerful transformation current

00:29:43.130 --> 00:29:44.550
of the 20th century.

00:29:44.550 --> 00:29:46.640
I'm illustrating here just a few products

00:29:46.640 --> 00:29:48.900
of the convergence of
physics with engineering,

00:29:48.900 --> 00:29:50.080
convergence 1.0.

00:29:50.979 --> 00:29:53.320
Great policy, important investments

00:29:53.320 --> 00:29:56.850
drove technology development
that fuels economic growth

00:29:56.850 --> 00:29:58.680
and these technologies permeate

00:29:58.680 --> 00:30:00.810
every nook and cranny of our lives.

00:30:00.810 --> 00:30:02.270
So the question is what's next?

00:30:02.270 --> 00:30:04.810
How do we defeat Malthus this time?

00:30:04.810 --> 00:30:07.220
Where will the future come from?

00:30:07.220 --> 00:30:09.920
And as I mentioned earlier,
there's another convergence

00:30:09.920 --> 00:30:12.520
that builds on this
20th century convergence

00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:15.960
of physics with engineering
adding biology into it.

00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:19.490
But biology, as late as 1950,

00:30:19.490 --> 00:30:21.360
needed several revolutions of its own

00:30:21.360 --> 00:30:22.780
in order to participate.

00:30:22.780 --> 00:30:27.030
Biology didn't have a parts list.

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:28.920
So the first revolution in biology

00:30:28.920 --> 00:30:30.470
that delivered the parts list

00:30:30.470 --> 00:30:32.993
was the revolution of molecular biology.

00:30:34.604 --> 00:30:37.280
Molecular biology transformed the way

00:30:37.280 --> 00:30:39.170
we thought about biology.

00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:42.090
It developed a biology parts list

00:30:42.090 --> 00:30:45.090
starting with the description
of the structure of DNA

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:47.920
and then the understanding that the code,

00:30:47.920 --> 00:30:50.970
the vocabulary of DNA, the words of DNA

00:30:50.970 --> 00:30:54.737
were translated into an
intermediary molecule called RNA.

00:30:55.804 --> 00:30:58.550
And that RNA then was the molecule

00:30:58.550 --> 00:31:01.960
that gave the directions
to construct proteins.

00:31:01.960 --> 00:31:04.030
There are many, many proteins,

00:31:04.030 --> 00:31:06.640
each very specific to a particular cell

00:31:06.640 --> 00:31:11.640
or tissue or organism but
having these concepts in hand

00:31:11.970 --> 00:31:16.320
gave biology the parts list it needed.

00:31:16.320 --> 00:31:18.330
Of course, we came to understand that DNA

00:31:18.330 --> 00:31:21.420
was the substance of heredity

00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:24.660
and we could identify
particular DNA segments

00:31:24.660 --> 00:31:27.290
that's sometimes were
responsible for a disease.

00:31:27.290 --> 00:31:31.490
We can now construct molecules
that intervened diseases

00:31:31.490 --> 00:31:36.160
by intervening at the level
of DNA or RNA or protein.

00:31:36.160 --> 00:31:39.980
It has been an extraordinarily productive

00:31:39.980 --> 00:31:44.573
set of discoveries, this parts
list of the biological world.

00:31:45.860 --> 00:31:50.860
Now, molecular biology
gave us the possibility

00:31:50.860 --> 00:31:53.740
to study genes one by one.

00:31:53.740 --> 00:31:57.351
Incredible insights but we
needed another revolution

00:31:57.351 --> 00:32:00.890
in a sense, industrialized biology

00:32:00.890 --> 00:32:05.890
and that second molecular
biology revolution is genomics.

00:32:06.040 --> 00:32:08.640
I'm showing here a graph that illustrates

00:32:08.640 --> 00:32:13.520
the declining cost of
sequencing a human genome.

00:32:13.520 --> 00:32:17.860
That is perhaps the most
important tool that we have

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:20.210
in studying biology and applying biology

00:32:20.210 --> 00:32:21.710
to other activities.

00:32:21.710 --> 00:32:24.880
I'm competing the cost of
the genome in the red line

00:32:24.880 --> 00:32:29.880
against Moore's law which is
the incredibly rapid increase

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:31.520
in the number of transistors

00:32:31.520 --> 00:32:32.890
that can fit on a computer chip,

00:32:32.890 --> 00:32:35.930
which is a technological
marvel in and of itself

00:32:35.930 --> 00:32:37.727
but the declining cost of a human genome,

00:32:37.727 --> 00:32:42.610
at least as I here, is even more amazing.

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:44.520
So the first human genome

00:32:44.520 --> 00:32:46.170
came out of the human the genome project,

00:32:46.170 --> 00:32:50.000
which was roughly between 1984 and 2003,

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:53.130
took about 10 years to
sequence the first human genome

00:32:53.130 --> 00:32:55.260
and depending on how you measure it,

00:32:55.260 --> 00:33:00.260
it costs between 100 and $150
million to sequence a genome.

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:05.060
This declining costs and increasing speed

00:33:05.060 --> 00:33:06.930
in sequencing a human genome,

00:33:06.930 --> 00:33:09.570
today, my colleagues
across the street from me

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:11.784
at the Broad Institute tell me

00:33:11.784 --> 00:33:16.630
that they can sequence the
human genome, not in 10 years,

00:33:16.630 --> 00:33:20.330
in six minutes and the
cost of that sequencing

00:33:20.330 --> 00:33:23.730
is well less than $1,000 a genome.

00:33:23.730 --> 00:33:25.870
An extraordinary advance.

00:33:25.870 --> 00:33:28.530
The point is that having the understanding

00:33:28.530 --> 00:33:32.100
of the components of molecular
biology and genomics,

00:33:32.100 --> 00:33:34.970
we now have a biology parts list.

00:33:34.970 --> 00:33:37.480
And when I arrived at MIT,

00:33:37.480 --> 00:33:41.960
I discovered that my marvelous
friends, the engineers,

00:33:41.960 --> 00:33:44.470
were picking up this new parts list

00:33:44.470 --> 00:33:48.060
and using it to build new technologies

00:33:48.060 --> 00:33:50.750
in ways that are entirely transformative

00:33:50.750 --> 00:33:52.570
and quite remarkable.

00:33:52.570 --> 00:33:55.670
So, the third the revolution
that I'm gonna talk about

00:33:55.670 --> 00:33:57.850
is, of course, is the convergence

00:33:57.850 --> 00:34:00.740
of biology with engineering,
convergence 2.0,

00:34:00.740 --> 00:34:02.320
and there are a number
of examples in the book.

00:34:02.320 --> 00:34:04.110
For today, I'm just gonna talk about two.

00:34:04.110 --> 00:34:06.230
I'm gonna give you an
example from healthcare,

00:34:06.230 --> 00:34:07.420
an example from energy.

00:34:07.420 --> 00:34:10.470
But the point of my book and
the point of today's lecture

00:34:10.470 --> 00:34:14.870
is that these new technologies
are emerging all around you

00:34:14.870 --> 00:34:17.980
and if I can just give you the translation

00:34:17.980 --> 00:34:20.986
that allows you to understand
that this convergence 2.0

00:34:20.986 --> 00:34:23.930
is happening all around,
you will see examples.

00:34:23.930 --> 00:34:25.720
You'll see examples when
you pick up a newspaper

00:34:25.720 --> 00:34:29.230
when you run into any description
of the science of today.

00:34:29.230 --> 00:34:31.003
So, let's get started.

00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:37.230
The first example comes from
my colleague, Sangeeta Bhatia,

00:34:37.230 --> 00:34:40.060
and I'm gonna be a little
briefer 'cause I see we're,

00:34:40.060 --> 00:34:40.999
I don't wanna run over,

00:34:40.999 --> 00:34:42.810
I wanna give you plenty
of time for questions.

00:34:42.810 --> 00:34:45.930
So Sangeeta Bhatia is a nanotechnologist,

00:34:45.930 --> 00:34:48.170
she's an engineer, she's also an MD

00:34:48.170 --> 00:34:51.520
and she's tackled a problem of detection.

00:34:51.520 --> 00:34:53.000
Now, you'll recall that we talked

00:34:53.000 --> 00:34:54.767
about how much money we spend on disease

00:34:54.767 --> 00:34:57.190
and if we use cancer as an example

00:34:57.190 --> 00:34:59.960
of just the inefficiency of our system,

00:34:59.960 --> 00:35:03.760
if you have cancer, well,
actually, let's just back up.

00:35:03.760 --> 00:35:06.420
What's the best way of battling cancer?

00:35:06.420 --> 00:35:08.330
The best way is not to get it

00:35:08.330 --> 00:35:11.992
and if you use the tools that we have,

00:35:11.992 --> 00:35:14.440
stop smoking, if you use sunscreen,

00:35:14.440 --> 00:35:17.230
I'm as guilty as anyone
else in not using sunscreen

00:35:17.230 --> 00:35:18.407
as often as I should,

00:35:18.407 --> 00:35:20.990
if you get the vaccines
against the viruses

00:35:20.990 --> 00:35:22.297
that we know to cause cancer,

00:35:22.297 --> 00:35:25.520
all of that will reduce
the amount of cancer

00:35:25.520 --> 00:35:27.270
in our population and indeed,

00:35:27.270 --> 00:35:29.960
the cessation of smoking
or the reduction in smoking

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:34.400
has had a dramatic effect on
the incidence of lung cancer.

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:37.280
However, even if you did
everything we know today

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:40.617
for many cancer, people still get cancer

00:35:40.617 --> 00:35:44.620
and the second best approach
for cancer is early detection.

00:35:44.620 --> 00:35:47.350
Cancer is a disease for
some one cell, then two,

00:35:47.350 --> 00:35:49.800
then four, then eight, these cells divide

00:35:49.800 --> 00:35:51.810
and the earlier you catch cancer,

00:35:51.810 --> 00:35:54.611
the earlier you can detect and remove

00:35:54.611 --> 00:35:56.990
a cancerous set of cells,

00:35:56.990 --> 00:35:59.350
the better chance you have at a cure.

00:35:59.350 --> 00:36:01.190
However, our current technologies,

00:36:01.190 --> 00:36:03.870
while they're much better
than they were before

00:36:03.870 --> 00:36:07.290
are still often insufficient
to detect a cancer

00:36:07.290 --> 00:36:09.040
at a small enough size

00:36:09.040 --> 00:36:12.240
or a cancer before it's moved
from its tissue of origin

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:14.070
to others before it's metastasized,

00:36:14.070 --> 00:36:16.660
which makes it a very
difficult disease to treat.

00:36:16.660 --> 00:36:20.490
So Sangeeta has decided to tackle this

00:36:20.490 --> 00:36:23.180
using the genius of biology.

00:36:23.180 --> 00:36:24.720
On the right side of the slide,

00:36:24.720 --> 00:36:27.060
and I didn't put my pointer up.

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:28.713
Let me see if I can put my pointer up.

00:36:29.680 --> 00:36:31.233
Yeah, hold on, one second.

00:36:32.350 --> 00:36:33.660
Let's see.

00:36:33.660 --> 00:36:35.200
I hope you can see that.

00:36:35.200 --> 00:36:38.030
On the right side of the
slide, this turquoise center,

00:36:38.030 --> 00:36:41.680
this is a nanoparticle,
about 15 nanometers in size,

00:36:41.680 --> 00:36:44.410
that Sangeeta has
decorated with a very short

00:36:44.410 --> 00:36:47.260
segment of protein and
that's shown in this gold

00:36:47.260 --> 00:36:49.880
with a little turquoise marker part

00:36:49.880 --> 00:36:52.108
of that protein on the end.

00:36:52.108 --> 00:36:57.108
Every cell, every tissue
has its own proteins

00:36:57.140 --> 00:37:00.607
and its own proteins
that cut other proteins

00:37:00.607 --> 00:37:02.290
and those are called enzymes.

00:37:02.290 --> 00:37:05.030
Tissue-specific or
disease-specific enzymes

00:37:05.030 --> 00:37:07.340
and that's illustrated
by this little Pac-Man,

00:37:07.340 --> 00:37:09.150
this gold Pac-Man character

00:37:09.150 --> 00:37:12.220
which represents a cancer enzyme.

00:37:12.220 --> 00:37:15.960
Now, with this nanoparticle
that Sangeeta has designed,

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:19.660
the idea is that if someone
is suspected of having cancer,

00:37:19.660 --> 00:37:22.050
they get injected with a nanoparticle

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:24.220
and if they don't have cancer,

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:27.230
it remains intact and
passes out of the body

00:37:27.230 --> 00:37:29.360
like other waste material.

00:37:29.360 --> 00:37:32.650
However, if cancer is
present and these enzymes,

00:37:32.650 --> 00:37:35.030
these cancer enzymes are present,

00:37:35.030 --> 00:37:40.030
these cancer enzymes will clip
these little protein tethers,

00:37:40.280 --> 00:37:42.130
these little peptide tethers,

00:37:42.130 --> 00:37:45.850
releasing the signal part of that protein,

00:37:45.850 --> 00:37:48.563
these little turquoise loops.

00:37:50.340 --> 00:37:54.210
Sangeeta has engineered
these loops to be very small.

00:37:54.210 --> 00:37:56.260
Small enough that they can find their way

00:37:56.260 --> 00:37:58.090
back into the bloodstream

00:37:58.090 --> 00:37:59.750
and once they're back in the bloodstream,

00:37:59.750 --> 00:38:02.050
they are so small that the kidney

00:38:02.910 --> 00:38:07.020
thinks their waste products
and passes them into the urine.

00:38:07.020 --> 00:38:11.170
So if you have cancer and
you were to get this test,

00:38:11.170 --> 00:38:15.150
we could gather your urine and
look where these turquoise,

00:38:15.150 --> 00:38:19.710
they're not turquoise in real
experiment but marker proteins

00:38:19.710 --> 00:38:21.210
and because the urine

00:38:21.210 --> 00:38:23.750
normally has a very, very low background

00:38:23.750 --> 00:38:25.583
of protein or any contaminant,

00:38:25.583 --> 00:38:28.663
you get a very profound signal to noise.

00:38:29.960 --> 00:38:31.810
Sangeeta starting to cut started a company

00:38:31.810 --> 00:38:35.790
called Glympse Bio to bring
this technology to the market

00:38:35.790 --> 00:38:38.440
and they are currently in clinical trials.

00:38:38.440 --> 00:38:40.310
In their animal models,

00:38:40.310 --> 00:38:44.940
this enzyme-mediated detection technology

00:38:44.940 --> 00:38:49.100
can detect tumors when
they are 1/10 the size

00:38:49.100 --> 00:38:52.610
of current best in
class detection methods.

00:38:52.610 --> 00:38:57.610
If we could detect cancer
when they're 1/10 of the size,

00:38:57.780 --> 00:39:01.433
we would have a very great
advantage on beating it.

00:39:02.420 --> 00:39:04.133
So these are the kinds of technologies

00:39:04.133 --> 00:39:06.350
that come out of a nanotechnologist lab

00:39:06.350 --> 00:39:08.640
but again, a nanotechnologist,

00:39:08.640 --> 00:39:10.730
which builds these little devices,

00:39:10.730 --> 00:39:14.170
these nanoparticles using nanotechnology

00:39:14.170 --> 00:39:15.790
but uses nature's genius,

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:20.790
the brilliant specificity
and efficiency of our enzymes

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:24.590
to produce a detection technique.

00:39:24.590 --> 00:39:26.020
I'm just gonna skip the next slide

00:39:26.020 --> 00:39:27.400
and I'll just say very briefly

00:39:27.400 --> 00:39:32.400
that this is an intravenous,
a bloodborne detection assay

00:39:33.030 --> 00:39:36.580
but Sangeeta has also
established this kind of assay

00:39:36.580 --> 00:39:41.580
has an inhaled assay to
detect lung diseases.

00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:44.173
For example, COVID.

00:39:45.170 --> 00:39:48.410
And here, the nanoparticles are inhaled.

00:39:48.410 --> 00:39:52.090
If you have the disease, the
little marker proteins here,

00:39:52.090 --> 00:39:54.620
they're in orange that cleaved
off and you exhale them

00:39:54.620 --> 00:39:57.095
which can be detected with a breathalyzer.

00:39:57.095 --> 00:39:59.010
The reason that this is for me,

00:39:59.010 --> 00:40:02.460
such a compelling direction

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:07.460
in such an impellingly important
concept of possibilities

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:10.210
that we desperately need.

00:40:10.210 --> 00:40:13.770
I gave the example for cancer

00:40:13.770 --> 00:40:17.980
but frankly, for me, one
of the most painful parts

00:40:17.980 --> 00:40:21.010
of watching COVID go
through our populations

00:40:21.010 --> 00:40:26.010
over the last year was
the lack of diagnostics.

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:29.550
We have a diagnostics
deficit in the country

00:40:29.550 --> 00:40:31.930
that's bad for cancer and it's terrible

00:40:31.930 --> 00:40:33.610
in the case of the pandemic.

00:40:33.610 --> 00:40:37.830
We need better diagnostics that
we can detect disease early

00:40:37.830 --> 00:40:42.040
as I've described, accurately, quickly.

00:40:42.040 --> 00:40:43.480
You should be able to understand

00:40:43.480 --> 00:40:45.770
whether you have COVID
or not within 15 minutes

00:40:45.770 --> 00:40:48.270
of walking into your provider's office,

00:40:48.270 --> 00:40:51.560
not after two days that are inexpensive,

00:40:51.560 --> 00:40:55.750
that are sensitive and that
can perform millions of tests.

00:40:55.750 --> 00:40:57.790
We don't have it yet and I'm hoping

00:40:57.790 --> 00:40:58.780
that coming out of COVID,

00:40:58.780 --> 00:41:01.820
we may actually get ourselves
organized around it.

00:41:01.820 --> 00:41:03.020
The next example I'm gonna give

00:41:03.020 --> 00:41:05.210
is from another colleague
at the Koch Institute,

00:41:05.210 --> 00:41:06.690
Angela Belcher.

00:41:06.690 --> 00:41:10.770
Angie is a materials engineer

00:41:10.770 --> 00:41:13.900
and in this picture on the left,

00:41:13.900 --> 00:41:15.590
that's Angie and in her hand,

00:41:15.590 --> 00:41:17.980
she's holding an abalone shell.

00:41:17.980 --> 00:41:20.780
The abalone shell is filled
with coin cells batteries

00:41:20.780 --> 00:41:22.230
and they look like the coin cell batteries

00:41:22.230 --> 00:41:24.520
that you might use to
change for your remote

00:41:24.520 --> 00:41:26.700
or any one of your small
electronic devices.

00:41:26.700 --> 00:41:29.470
However, these just look like
those coin cell batteries

00:41:29.470 --> 00:41:31.440
because they're filled
with a new kind of battery

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:34.690
that Angie has developed and are batteries

00:41:34.690 --> 00:41:36.970
that are made by nature.

00:41:36.970 --> 00:41:40.350
When Angie was a student
at UC Santa Barbara,

00:41:40.350 --> 00:41:42.450
she used to walk along the ocean's edge,

00:41:42.450 --> 00:41:44.620
loved the ocean's edge
but what she really loved

00:41:44.620 --> 00:41:47.820
were these abalone shells
because she reasoned

00:41:47.820 --> 00:41:49.730
that abalone, a sea creature,

00:41:49.730 --> 00:41:54.350
picks off the materials from
the sea it lives in around it

00:41:54.350 --> 00:41:58.920
and built an abalone shell which
is the technology it needs.

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:01.863
It's strong, it's
lightweight and best of all,

00:42:01.863 --> 00:42:05.380
when the abalone dies, an
abalone shell disintegrates

00:42:05.380 --> 00:42:07.840
into its component parts
so that the next abalone

00:42:07.840 --> 00:42:09.910
can build its shell or
another sea creature

00:42:09.910 --> 00:42:11.860
can use those components.

00:42:11.860 --> 00:42:13.130
And Angie thought,

00:42:13.130 --> 00:42:16.640
you know, if abalone can
build the technology they need

00:42:16.640 --> 00:42:20.123
without contaminant their
world, why can't we?

00:42:21.010 --> 00:42:26.010
So Angie's work is devoted
to using nature's tools

00:42:26.510 --> 00:42:30.060
to build technologies that are better.

00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:31.900
And as I described at the beginning,

00:42:31.900 --> 00:42:34.070
we have an enormous energy problem

00:42:34.070 --> 00:42:35.843
and that's gonna get much worse.

00:42:37.110 --> 00:42:39.700
And we have to figure
out how to address it.

00:42:39.700 --> 00:42:42.750
Now, people are enthusiastic
about alternative energy.

00:42:42.750 --> 00:42:44.141
I love wind I love solar too

00:42:44.141 --> 00:42:46.530
but the rate limiting technology

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:48.410
for getting out of our energy dilemma,

00:42:48.410 --> 00:42:51.810
frankly, is not bigger or wind turbines

00:42:51.810 --> 00:42:53.795
or larger solar farms.

00:42:53.795 --> 00:42:57.080
The problem, the rate limiting technology

00:42:57.080 --> 00:42:59.840
is energy storage, batteries.

00:42:59.840 --> 00:43:02.890
We've made a lot of improvement
in battery technology

00:43:02.890 --> 00:43:05.810
but frankly, state-of-the-art
lithium-ion batteries

00:43:05.810 --> 00:43:08.450
are not the end of the story for us.

00:43:08.450 --> 00:43:09.810
They can't be.

00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:12.710
In order to manufacture
lithium-ion batteries,

00:43:12.710 --> 00:43:14.650
you need an enormous amount of heat.

00:43:14.650 --> 00:43:17.870
It's a very, very intensive heat process

00:43:17.870 --> 00:43:20.080
so it consumes a lot of energy

00:43:20.080 --> 00:43:23.550
and there are a lot of toxic byproducts.

00:43:23.550 --> 00:43:27.060
So unless we figure out
how to build batteries

00:43:27.060 --> 00:43:30.160
more sustainably, we are
not gonna get very far

00:43:30.160 --> 00:43:32.800
with the best alternative
energy strategies

00:43:32.800 --> 00:43:34.020
that we can imagine.

00:43:34.020 --> 00:43:36.310
So Angie has done a number of things

00:43:36.310 --> 00:43:37.910
but for the battery application,

00:43:37.910 --> 00:43:40.859
she has used a small bacteriophage,

00:43:40.859 --> 00:43:43.800
a virus that infects
bacteria and it showed here,

00:43:43.800 --> 00:43:46.923
it's called the M13 virus.

00:43:48.717 --> 00:43:53.717
And she described that she's
caused nature to evolve faster.

00:43:55.210 --> 00:43:59.650
Viruses normally bind living organisms,

00:43:59.650 --> 00:44:02.680
material from living
cells and Angie's question

00:44:02.680 --> 00:44:06.370
is could she persuade these M13 viruses

00:44:06.370 --> 00:44:08.573
to bind battery materials?

00:44:09.720 --> 00:44:13.000
Inorganic materials, and
indeed, that's what she's done.

00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:15.610
I'm just gonna show you a
slide just to illustrate

00:44:15.610 --> 00:44:18.210
how these batteries are built.

00:44:18.210 --> 00:44:20.980
So here, over on the left is an M13.

00:44:20.980 --> 00:44:25.021
You see it's quite small,
less than 10 nanometers across

00:44:25.021 --> 00:44:28.230
and it has a very the
repetitive structure.

00:44:28.230 --> 00:44:32.290
So there are proteins called
p8 that forms the core.

00:44:32.290 --> 00:44:36.480
The tuft of these viruses
formed by a protein called p3

00:44:36.480 --> 00:44:41.180
and she has modified the p3 proteins

00:44:41.180 --> 00:44:44.270
so that they bind
single-walled carbon nanotubes,

00:44:44.270 --> 00:44:47.280
which are very good
conductors of electricity

00:44:47.280 --> 00:44:50.840
or in some cases, cobalt,
magnesium, lithium,

00:44:50.840 --> 00:44:52.823
the components for batteries.

00:44:53.760 --> 00:44:56.640
Once these viruses have been modified,

00:44:56.640 --> 00:44:57.760
they're kind of crystalline

00:44:57.760 --> 00:45:01.540
so they pack together very
closely the way crystals do

00:45:01.540 --> 00:45:04.840
and then she uses them to build cathodes.

00:45:04.840 --> 00:45:07.130
She also, this case, in this illustration,

00:45:07.130 --> 00:45:09.020
the anode is a lithium foil

00:45:09.020 --> 00:45:11.987
but she's also used virus-built anodes.

00:45:13.264 --> 00:45:17.230
She packs them together into
these coin cell battery cases

00:45:17.230 --> 00:45:19.580
and they looked just
like any old coin cell

00:45:19.580 --> 00:45:23.110
but they're not because
they're built from nature.

00:45:23.110 --> 00:45:28.110
Most importantly, her battery
manufacturing technology

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:31.210
builds these batteries at room temperature

00:45:31.210 --> 00:45:33.550
without any toxic byproducts.

00:45:33.550 --> 00:45:35.460
If we could build the batteries we need

00:45:35.460 --> 00:45:38.880
using Angie's technology,
we would have a chance

00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:43.813
to get out of our dilemma of
having insufficient energy.

00:45:44.764 --> 00:45:48.500
So, two examples that I think

00:45:48.500 --> 00:45:53.470
are just fabulous
illustrations of using nature

00:45:53.470 --> 00:45:55.713
to build better.

00:45:56.810 --> 00:46:01.810
So, now, these convergence
things are easy to describe

00:46:03.070 --> 00:46:05.130
but they are hard to do.

00:46:05.130 --> 00:46:08.650
Why is it hard to implement
and accelerate convergence?

00:46:08.650 --> 00:46:10.090
The first is the innovator's dilemma.

00:46:10.090 --> 00:46:11.420
If we think about our university

00:46:11.420 --> 00:46:13.560
as just in terms of making it possible

00:46:13.560 --> 00:46:15.840
for people to talk across disciplines,

00:46:15.840 --> 00:46:18.030
our university worked pretty darn well.

00:46:18.030 --> 00:46:19.200
And if you're gonna be a chemist,

00:46:19.200 --> 00:46:21.080
it's important that you learn chemistry,

00:46:21.080 --> 00:46:22.810
probably more important
than you learn chemistry

00:46:22.810 --> 00:46:25.130
than you understand how
semiconductors work.

00:46:25.130 --> 00:46:28.340
However, this is a problem
in terms of advancing

00:46:28.340 --> 00:46:31.160
into our next world

00:46:31.160 --> 00:46:34.216
and the challenges across
disciplines makes sense.

00:46:34.216 --> 00:46:36.500
Disciplines use different languages,

00:46:36.500 --> 00:46:38.000
they use different approaches

00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:40.850
and of course, there're
any number of structures,

00:46:40.850 --> 00:46:42.900
institutional structures
that make it difficult

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:47.040
for people to communicate
across disciplines

00:46:47.040 --> 00:46:49.708
and our funding agencies are siloed.

00:46:49.708 --> 00:46:54.370
This, to me, has been a really
very, very difficult problem.

00:46:54.370 --> 00:46:56.490
Fabulous for Vannevar Bush and have said

00:46:56.490 --> 00:46:58.240
let's set up these funding agencies

00:46:58.240 --> 00:47:00.440
but the fact is that the
National Institutes of Health

00:47:00.440 --> 00:47:04.350
has done a fabulous job at
funding biology and biomedicine,

00:47:04.350 --> 00:47:06.220
the NSF has done a fabulous job

00:47:06.220 --> 00:47:09.490
at funding computer
science and engineering

00:47:09.490 --> 00:47:10.510
and the Department of Energy

00:47:10.510 --> 00:47:13.080
has done a fabulous
job at funding physics.

00:47:13.080 --> 00:47:15.530
Just the question is how
do you provide funding

00:47:15.530 --> 00:47:18.300
for activities that cross disciplines?

00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:20.040
Once in a while, we've
gotten this together.

00:47:20.040 --> 00:47:22.300
There was a National
Nanotechnology Initiative

00:47:22.300 --> 00:47:24.810
that brought together
four different agencies

00:47:24.810 --> 00:47:28.060
and really propelled the United
States from being a laggard

00:47:28.060 --> 00:47:30.320
to a leader in nanotechnology.

00:47:30.320 --> 00:47:31.538
The current brain project

00:47:31.538 --> 00:47:34.510
also brings together
many different agencies

00:47:34.510 --> 00:47:38.340
to solve the some of the
really daunting problems

00:47:38.340 --> 00:47:40.570
of decoding the brain.

00:47:40.570 --> 00:47:42.330
But the fact is those are exceptions,

00:47:42.330 --> 00:47:44.240
they're not the rule
and we need to be able

00:47:44.240 --> 00:47:47.870
to do cross disciplinary funding and work

00:47:47.870 --> 00:47:51.833
without inventing a new
process to do so every time.

00:47:52.678 --> 00:47:54.070
I'm gonna give you just one example

00:47:54.070 --> 00:47:55.930
and I'll go through
this relatively quickly

00:47:55.930 --> 00:47:58.530
of one of our convergence efforts at MIT.

00:47:58.530 --> 00:48:01.476
This is the Koch Institute for
Integrative Cancer Research.

00:48:01.476 --> 00:48:06.476
We put it together in 2007,
the building was done in 2011

00:48:07.169 --> 00:48:09.480
and I have to say there are several things

00:48:09.480 --> 00:48:13.020
that make this work.

00:48:13.020 --> 00:48:16.280
The first is that everyone in the building

00:48:16.280 --> 00:48:20.523
shares the ambition to accelerate
progress against cancer.

00:48:21.924 --> 00:48:26.924
And the second is that
there is protected space.

00:48:27.384 --> 00:48:30.780
All of the faculty in the Koch Institute

00:48:30.780 --> 00:48:34.310
have their faculty appointments
in their departments.

00:48:34.310 --> 00:48:37.190
In biology, in mechanical engineering,

00:48:37.190 --> 00:48:40.240
in the department of material
sciences and engineering

00:48:40.240 --> 00:48:42.470
and they just cohabitate
for their research

00:48:42.470 --> 00:48:44.970
so we didn't have to
change the academic process

00:48:44.970 --> 00:48:46.350
of faculty appointments.

00:48:46.350 --> 00:48:48.253
That really allowed us to accelerate.

00:48:49.200 --> 00:48:52.140
And the third element that is
absolutely critical success

00:48:52.140 --> 00:48:53.660
is we had seed funding

00:48:53.660 --> 00:48:56.580
outside of standard funding practices.

00:48:56.580 --> 00:48:58.040
What do I mean?

00:48:58.040 --> 00:49:01.100
Private philanthropy, enormous generosity

00:49:01.100 --> 00:49:02.550
by a number of philanthropists

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:06.030
that allowed us to get started and prove

00:49:06.030 --> 00:49:09.010
what we said we were gonna do which then

00:49:09.010 --> 00:49:10.630
allowed us to get funding for this

00:49:10.630 --> 00:49:12.350
from the standard agencies.

00:49:12.350 --> 00:49:14.323
But that was absolutely essential.

00:49:15.270 --> 00:49:17.640
And we built this building

00:49:17.640 --> 00:49:19.570
and established this Institute

00:49:20.440 --> 00:49:21.613
with the determination

00:49:21.613 --> 00:49:25.357
that we were going to foster collaboration

00:49:25.357 --> 00:49:28.090
and we engineered with four
layers of collaboration.

00:49:28.090 --> 00:49:30.810
Up here in the top left,
chosen original floor plan

00:49:30.810 --> 00:49:32.650
of one of the floors of the building.

00:49:32.650 --> 00:49:34.250
All the floors are mixed.

00:49:34.250 --> 00:49:35.520
With engineering and biology,

00:49:35.520 --> 00:49:37.610
we want to encourage
people to talk together

00:49:37.610 --> 00:49:40.030
and this was the early plan and now,

00:49:40.030 --> 00:49:42.050
as faculty have left and
new faculty have come,

00:49:42.050 --> 00:49:43.390
it's actually more integrated

00:49:43.390 --> 00:49:44.760
that you don't have a biology half

00:49:44.760 --> 00:49:46.730
and engineering half of a floor.

00:49:46.730 --> 00:49:49.690
Importantly, the building
has one set of stairs,

00:49:49.690 --> 00:49:52.330
one set of elevators,
one set of restrooms,

00:49:52.330 --> 00:49:54.810
one cafe so wherever you go,

00:49:54.810 --> 00:49:56.430
you're likely to run into someone

00:49:56.430 --> 00:49:59.240
who comes from the other
side of the equation

00:49:59.240 --> 00:50:01.200
and that's been enormously helpful.

00:50:01.200 --> 00:50:02.840
We situated the Koch Institute

00:50:02.840 --> 00:50:05.820
among a number of other disciplines.

00:50:05.820 --> 00:50:08.050
Obviously, next to the biology building,

00:50:08.050 --> 00:50:10.100
the Stata Center is the home

00:50:10.100 --> 00:50:12.160
of electrical engineering,
computer science,

00:50:12.160 --> 00:50:13.250
right across the street

00:50:13.250 --> 00:50:16.230
are the Broad Institute and
the Whitehead Institute,

00:50:16.230 --> 00:50:19.430
chemical engineering is here,
chemistry is right here.

00:50:19.430 --> 00:50:22.890
It makes it very easy as
you crossed this courtyard,

00:50:22.890 --> 00:50:25.440
again, to run into people
who aren't exactly like you

00:50:25.440 --> 00:50:27.250
and easy to start those conversations

00:50:27.250 --> 00:50:29.323
that might otherwise be difficult.

00:50:30.200 --> 00:50:32.770
You all are certainly aware that MIT

00:50:32.770 --> 00:50:35.270
does not have a medical
school or a hospital.

00:50:35.270 --> 00:50:37.300
This is the third layer of collaboration.

00:50:37.300 --> 00:50:38.900
We have to establish collaboration

00:50:38.900 --> 00:50:40.770
with our clinical partners.

00:50:40.770 --> 00:50:42.100
Mass General Hospital,

00:50:42.100 --> 00:50:44.550
here, down here,
Dana-Farber/Brigham and Women's,

00:50:44.550 --> 00:50:45.470
Children's Hospital.

00:50:45.470 --> 00:50:49.720
We have great hospitals in
our region, just not at MIT,

00:50:49.720 --> 00:50:51.610
and we have something
called the bridge project

00:50:51.610 --> 00:50:53.160
that brings together clinicians

00:50:53.160 --> 00:50:56.617
with a clinical awareness
and understanding of cancer

00:50:56.617 --> 00:50:59.760
along with our biologists and engineers.

00:50:59.760 --> 00:51:01.430
And of course, I would be telling you

00:51:01.430 --> 00:51:03.702
only part of the story
if I didn't remind you

00:51:03.702 --> 00:51:07.090
that MIT, which are these
buildings shown in red

00:51:07.090 --> 00:51:10.720
are embedded in the Kendall
Square Innovation District.

00:51:10.720 --> 00:51:13.510
My colleague, the Nobel
Prize winner, Phil Sharp,

00:51:13.510 --> 00:51:18.510
often says that technology
travels on two feet and it does.

00:51:19.190 --> 00:51:22.100
And so, it is to say
we've lowered the barriers

00:51:22.100 --> 00:51:24.340
for translating great discoveries

00:51:24.340 --> 00:51:28.140
into marketplace lifesaving
products by where we sit

00:51:28.140 --> 00:51:31.140
because if your office is
here in the Koch Institute,

00:51:31.140 --> 00:51:33.010
you don't have to walk too far to talk

00:51:33.010 --> 00:51:37.393
to venture capitalists and
people in the industry community.

00:51:38.560 --> 00:51:41.500
So, does it work?

00:51:41.500 --> 00:51:43.400
This is just one way you measure it.

00:51:43.400 --> 00:51:47.120
Obviously, there are more
grants and more publications

00:51:47.120 --> 00:51:50.790
but because we were
interested in implementation,

00:51:50.790 --> 00:51:52.930
we had kept very close
track of the companies

00:51:52.930 --> 00:51:54.317
that have started out
in the Koch Institute.

00:51:54.317 --> 00:51:55.630
And you can see back here

00:51:55.630 --> 00:51:58.524
when we started the
Koch Institute in 2007,

00:51:58.524 --> 00:52:00.290
a few companies were being started

00:52:00.290 --> 00:52:04.050
and we now have more than 100 companies

00:52:04.050 --> 00:52:07.020
that have been founded have
worked at the Koch Institute.

00:52:07.020 --> 00:52:09.180
Among those companies,
at least 30 of them now

00:52:09.180 --> 00:52:10.870
have products in clinical trials.

00:52:10.870 --> 00:52:12.588
So we feel that we're really delivering

00:52:12.588 --> 00:52:16.513
on our ambition to accelerate
progress against cancer.

00:52:17.690 --> 00:52:22.680
So, what is convergence 2.0 good for?

00:52:22.680 --> 00:52:25.080
I would say just about anything.

00:52:25.080 --> 00:52:26.961
And I've given some examples here

00:52:26.961 --> 00:52:29.620
and we talked about some of the challenges

00:52:29.620 --> 00:52:34.260
of the 21st century and the
importance of tackling them

00:52:34.260 --> 00:52:37.470
in a way that will not
just solve these problems

00:52:37.470 --> 00:52:40.930
but in the best of scenarios,

00:52:40.930 --> 00:52:44.700
also drive the kind of
industrial and economic growth

00:52:44.700 --> 00:52:47.720
that we enjoyed in the second
half of the 20th century.

00:52:47.720 --> 00:52:49.990
So, the answer to the Malthusian dilemma,

00:52:49.990 --> 00:52:52.500
of course, is more innovation.

00:52:52.500 --> 00:52:57.500
But we have to invest massively
in innovation for it to work

00:52:58.620 --> 00:53:01.190
and that is always suspect.

00:53:01.190 --> 00:53:02.860
Research is inefficient,

00:53:02.860 --> 00:53:05.510
you never know where it's
going when you embark on it

00:53:05.510 --> 00:53:09.810
and since the early part of the,

00:53:09.810 --> 00:53:11.270
second half of the 20th century

00:53:11.270 --> 00:53:14.470
where we got onto the Vannevar Bush plan,

00:53:14.470 --> 00:53:17.844
we have invested
significant federal funds.

00:53:17.844 --> 00:53:19.710
They have to be federal funds

00:53:19.710 --> 00:53:23.660
because the process of
innovation is just too slow

00:53:23.660 --> 00:53:26.200
for a company to invest
in this kind of work.

00:53:26.200 --> 00:53:28.220
They will invest later on when an idea

00:53:28.220 --> 00:53:31.030
is beginning to end mature
but not at the outset

00:53:31.030 --> 00:53:33.310
and this is the responsibility
of the federal government.

00:53:33.310 --> 00:53:35.580
However, it is always doubted.

00:53:35.580 --> 00:53:38.280
This is a story that may be apocryphal.

00:53:38.280 --> 00:53:42.110
Apparently, the then equivalent
secretary of the treasury,

00:53:42.110 --> 00:53:44.397
the Chancellor of the
Exchequer, William Gladstone,

00:53:44.397 --> 00:53:47.850
asked Faraday why he was bothering to do

00:53:47.850 --> 00:53:50.330
his kind of bumbling
around in the laboratory.

00:53:50.330 --> 00:53:51.580
What was the worth of it?

00:53:53.130 --> 00:53:55.827
And Faraday has reported to have replied,

00:53:55.827 --> 00:53:58.420
"Why sir, there's every probability

00:53:58.420 --> 00:54:00.391
that you will soon be able to tax it."

00:54:00.391 --> 00:54:03.260
And indeed, when I bought my last iPhone,

00:54:03.260 --> 00:54:06.030
I did pay some tax on that.

00:54:06.030 --> 00:54:09.260
It is the route to economic
and industrial progress

00:54:09.260 --> 00:54:11.780
but we have to feel it
from the very beginning.

00:54:11.780 --> 00:54:16.780
So, Bob Solow, an MIT
economist did the great study

00:54:16.980 --> 00:54:19.510
that showed that over
50% of economic growth

00:54:19.510 --> 00:54:22.560
since World War II is due to technology,

00:54:22.560 --> 00:54:27.400
not the standard
components of the economy.

00:54:27.400 --> 00:54:30.990
A very important study that
demonstrated the real product

00:54:30.990 --> 00:54:34.600
how much a nation can gain

00:54:34.600 --> 00:54:38.280
by that investing in the
raw material of innovation.

00:54:38.280 --> 00:54:39.553
So, how are we doing?

00:54:40.820 --> 00:54:44.070
This is a diagram showing in red

00:54:44.070 --> 00:54:49.057
are investments since 1953 in R&amp;D.

00:54:50.890 --> 00:54:52.830
I'm showing both the federal funds

00:54:52.830 --> 00:54:56.130
and also business funds in blue

00:54:56.130 --> 00:54:59.530
and I just wanna remind you
that the business funding side

00:54:59.530 --> 00:55:01.280
is more on the development side

00:55:01.280 --> 00:55:04.365
of the research and development, R&amp;D side

00:55:04.365 --> 00:55:08.460
but it's the research, the
fundamental research side

00:55:08.460 --> 00:55:11.510
that the federal government
has been responsible for.

00:55:11.510 --> 00:55:12.550
This graph shows it

00:55:12.550 --> 00:55:15.160
as a kind of a national
commitment to this effort

00:55:15.160 --> 00:55:18.660
and by demonstrating the percent of GDP.

00:55:18.660 --> 00:55:23.410
We got started in 50s, reached
the peak in the mid 1960s

00:55:23.410 --> 00:55:25.570
and have then tailed off.

00:55:25.570 --> 00:55:27.650
This graph doesn't go all
the way to the present day

00:55:27.650 --> 00:55:31.410
but today, it's about 0.7 or 0.8% of GDP

00:55:31.410 --> 00:55:35.020
that goes into this kind of
investment in our future.

00:55:35.020 --> 00:55:36.517
And you can say well,
it's still a lot of money

00:55:36.517 --> 00:55:38.690
and it is a lot of money.

00:55:38.690 --> 00:55:40.310
Of course, won't surprise you

00:55:40.310 --> 00:55:44.049
that I think never enough money
for this particular effort.

00:55:44.049 --> 00:55:47.230
And after World War II, we
were in this game by ourselves.

00:55:47.230 --> 00:55:49.370
The countries that could have competed

00:55:49.370 --> 00:55:51.150
were putting all the money they had

00:55:51.150 --> 00:55:53.760
into rebuilding their cities
and their countrysides

00:55:53.760 --> 00:55:56.060
that had been decimated during the war.

00:55:56.060 --> 00:55:58.400
United States was in a
very different place.

00:55:58.400 --> 00:56:01.440
We had the, let's say, the luxury

00:56:01.440 --> 00:56:05.190
of investing in our future
and the world's future.

00:56:05.190 --> 00:56:06.630
But that's no longer the case.

00:56:06.630 --> 00:56:08.343
There's a lot of competition out there

00:56:08.343 --> 00:56:10.914
and you can say that's
a good thing and it is.

00:56:10.914 --> 00:56:12.560
Here are the numbers

00:56:12.560 --> 00:56:15.290
from a number of countries
around the world.

00:56:15.290 --> 00:56:19.660
And you can see already that
South Korea, Japan and Germany

00:56:19.660 --> 00:56:24.660
invest a greater percentage
of their GDP in research.

00:56:25.003 --> 00:56:26.790
The line I worry about a lot, of course,

00:56:26.790 --> 00:56:28.740
is this purple line, China.

00:56:28.740 --> 00:56:31.010
And I don't know whether this purple line

00:56:31.010 --> 00:56:32.470
has yet crossed the red line.

00:56:32.470 --> 00:56:35.700
My guess is it will have
already done so last month

00:56:35.700 --> 00:56:37.180
or if it hasn't already done so,

00:56:37.180 --> 00:56:39.033
it'll be next month or next year.

00:56:40.020 --> 00:56:45.020
The way the United States
has managed our technology

00:56:45.659 --> 00:56:48.460
and come to the position that we have

00:56:48.460 --> 00:56:51.030
through the 20th century is not a secret.

00:56:51.030 --> 00:56:53.250
It's there for all the world to see

00:56:53.250 --> 00:56:56.780
and all the world is,
as rapidly as they can,

00:56:56.780 --> 00:56:59.440
following our pattern.

00:56:59.440 --> 00:57:04.440
So if we wanna be a player,
a major player going forward,

00:57:04.740 --> 00:57:09.340
we have to continue to
make these investments.

00:57:09.340 --> 00:57:14.110
So, there are things
about our federal policy

00:57:14.110 --> 00:57:15.940
that, I think, are essential

00:57:15.940 --> 00:57:18.490
to building this incredibly
important resource

00:57:18.490 --> 00:57:19.870
for the country.

00:57:19.870 --> 00:57:22.230
We need to have sustained
federal investments

00:57:22.230 --> 00:57:23.950
in basic research.

00:57:23.950 --> 00:57:25.560
I paid attention to this, of course,

00:57:25.560 --> 00:57:28.191
as soon as I started my own
lab where there were rich years

00:57:28.191 --> 00:57:30.860
or more generous years and poor years

00:57:30.860 --> 00:57:34.240
in terms of getting federal
grants to do our research

00:57:34.240 --> 00:57:36.450
and nothing could be more debilitating

00:57:36.450 --> 00:57:38.630
to a young investigator
than not to be able

00:57:38.630 --> 00:57:41.690
to get the funding to
support her or his research

00:57:42.550 --> 00:57:44.910
because of the vagaries in funding.

00:57:44.910 --> 00:57:47.440
Funding needs to be consistent.

00:57:47.440 --> 00:57:51.460
Very important for this convergence 2.0

00:57:51.460 --> 00:57:54.150
and for whatever
convergence 3.0 is gonna be

00:57:54.150 --> 00:57:56.450
is that we have flexible funds available

00:57:56.450 --> 00:57:58.240
for cross agency collaborations

00:57:58.240 --> 00:58:00.550
to fund the kind of work
that I've described today

00:58:00.550 --> 00:58:02.650
and then I have in my book.

00:58:02.650 --> 00:58:06.297
Importantly, when an interesting
idea gets out of the lab

00:58:06.297 --> 00:58:08.810
and is ready to go to the marketplace,

00:58:08.810 --> 00:58:10.140
we need policies

00:58:10.140 --> 00:58:13.300
that better support
investments in innovation.

00:58:13.300 --> 00:58:18.263
We do have a robust investment mechanism

00:58:19.140 --> 00:58:20.810
in the United States but I would say

00:58:20.810 --> 00:58:23.570
we could do more to encourage individuals

00:58:23.570 --> 00:58:28.570
and investors to put their
money into this kind of work.

00:58:28.640 --> 00:58:31.490
And it may sound weird coming from MIT

00:58:31.490 --> 00:58:34.970
where we're known fairly
well for our ability

00:58:34.970 --> 00:58:37.390
to tech to transfer
technology from the lab

00:58:37.390 --> 00:58:39.710
to the marketplace but even in MIT,

00:58:39.710 --> 00:58:41.795
I think we can do a better
job of making it easier,

00:58:41.795 --> 00:58:43.830
making it faster

00:58:43.830 --> 00:58:46.040
'cause these industry
university collaborations

00:58:46.040 --> 00:58:51.040
are critically important
to get across the gap

00:58:51.930 --> 00:58:56.400
between a laboratory discovery
and a marketplace technology

00:58:56.400 --> 00:58:58.400
that's gonna save a life.

00:58:58.400 --> 00:59:03.400
So, I've described you the
products of the convergence

00:59:03.670 --> 00:59:04.720
or some of the products.

00:59:04.720 --> 00:59:08.090
At least, the thesis
behind convergence 2.0

00:59:08.090 --> 00:59:11.240
which, I think, will
be the technology story

00:59:11.240 --> 00:59:13.150
of the 21st century.

00:59:13.150 --> 00:59:17.060
It's a story of fundamental research

00:59:17.060 --> 00:59:18.440
that moves to applications

00:59:18.440 --> 00:59:21.420
that move to new businesses
and new industries.

00:59:21.420 --> 00:59:26.420
And you know, we simply
must defeat Malthus again.

00:59:28.990 --> 00:59:33.113
But my very greatest hope
is that defeat of Malthus,

00:59:34.200 --> 00:59:38.541
I hope, will come not
from the threat of war

00:59:38.541 --> 00:59:41.553
but by the promise of peace.

00:59:42.450 --> 00:59:44.640
So my book gives you a front row seat

00:59:44.640 --> 00:59:46.490
on some of the most amazing,

00:59:46.490 --> 00:59:48.030
almost science fiction technologies

00:59:48.030 --> 00:59:49.560
coming out of labs right now.

00:59:49.560 --> 00:59:51.790
As I said, it's written
for a general audience

00:59:51.790 --> 00:59:55.260
and I just hope everyone
can enjoy the excitement

00:59:55.260 --> 00:59:58.900
of this generation's technology revolution

00:59:58.900 --> 01:00:01.240
and to understand it and I hope you will.

01:00:01.240 --> 01:00:04.070
Thanks and I'll take some questions now.

01:00:04.070 --> 01:00:08.120
- Thank you so much, Susan,
for sharing your vision

01:00:08.120 --> 01:00:12.490
and I am delighted that our president,

01:00:12.490 --> 01:00:13.600
president of Yale, Peter Salovey,

01:00:13.600 --> 01:00:15.720
a former friend and colleague of yours

01:00:16.620 --> 01:00:19.140
would love to ask the first question.

01:00:19.140 --> 01:00:20.003
So, Peter.

01:00:21.060 --> 01:00:22.030
- Hey, Peter.

01:00:22.030 --> 01:00:25.120
- While that's happening,
it's great to see you, Susan,

01:00:25.120 --> 01:00:27.770
and Priya, thank you for hosting us

01:00:27.770 --> 01:00:29.710
and for calling on me to
ask the first question.

01:00:29.710 --> 01:00:30.590
That's very nice of you.

01:00:30.590 --> 01:00:32.790
Tamar, that was a great introduction.

01:00:32.790 --> 01:00:34.750
And Rich and Barbara Franke,

01:00:34.750 --> 01:00:37.230
thank you so much for enabling all of this

01:00:37.230 --> 01:00:40.343
and so much else that you do for Yale.

01:00:41.810 --> 01:00:44.250
Just before I ask the question,

01:00:44.250 --> 01:00:47.190
it's great to see about
100 people with us today

01:00:47.190 --> 01:00:50.760
and one thing I did want to acknowledge

01:00:50.760 --> 01:00:53.890
is that Susan really is my
mentor as an administrator.

01:00:53.890 --> 01:00:58.890
And back in January of 2003,
it's really December 2002,

01:00:59.210 --> 01:01:02.470
Rick Levin asked me to be
dean of the graduate school

01:01:02.470 --> 01:01:04.302
when Susan moved over
to the provost's office

01:01:04.302 --> 01:01:08.410
and he gave me about a half
a day to think about it

01:01:08.410 --> 01:01:09.440
and about

01:01:11.300 --> 01:01:15.110
a week to get trained up and start

01:01:15.110 --> 01:01:20.110
and Susan was always someone
who was kind of a role model

01:01:21.240 --> 01:01:24.850
and then who tutored me
during that holiday break

01:01:24.850 --> 01:01:27.530
and got me tuned up to become a dean.

01:01:27.530 --> 01:01:31.530
And I had no idea where that
was going to lead either

01:01:31.530 --> 01:01:34.820
and at the time, I just wanted to see

01:01:34.820 --> 01:01:36.860
if I could get through January 2003

01:01:36.860 --> 01:01:41.050
without doing something foolish and,

01:01:41.050 --> 01:01:44.260
but Susan was a great, great teacher

01:01:44.260 --> 01:01:47.190
in this other area of university life

01:01:47.190 --> 01:01:48.943
and I'm very thankful for that.

01:01:51.970 --> 01:01:54.410
We're trying here at Yale to do

01:01:54.410 --> 01:01:57.100
some of the things that you have suggested

01:01:58.040 --> 01:02:01.278
that we do in order to create convergence

01:02:01.278 --> 01:02:03.793
and produce change in the world.

01:02:04.790 --> 01:02:07.220
We're also co-locating faculty

01:02:07.220 --> 01:02:09.240
across disciplinary boundaries.

01:02:09.240 --> 01:02:12.030
So, in the humanities,
you could see people

01:02:12.030 --> 01:02:14.090
coming together in our new quadrangle

01:02:14.090 --> 01:02:17.500
and in the Woodside Institute
at 100 College Street,

01:02:17.500 --> 01:02:19.820
you can see neuroscientists
and psychologists

01:02:19.820 --> 01:02:21.240
and computer scientists

01:02:21.240 --> 01:02:25.023
and some data science all converging.

01:02:26.787 --> 01:02:28.880
What used to be called Kline Biology Tower

01:02:28.880 --> 01:02:33.330
is now the Klein Tower of
data science, astronomy,

01:02:33.330 --> 01:02:34.280
mathematics, right?

01:02:34.280 --> 01:02:37.390
So we're doing all this
co-location and certainly,

01:02:37.390 --> 01:02:41.230
where we're trying to engage
philanthropists as you suggest

01:02:41.230 --> 01:02:43.780
and we're doing more
industrial collaborations

01:02:43.780 --> 01:02:47.160
with industry as you suggest.

01:02:47.160 --> 01:02:49.560
And particularly for Yale, that was a new,

01:02:49.560 --> 01:02:51.610
has been a bit more of a new adventure

01:02:51.610 --> 01:02:54.090
and has been a successful one.

01:02:54.090 --> 01:02:57.783
So my question is should we go further?

01:02:58.952 --> 01:03:01.820
I'm not suggesting this seriously

01:03:01.820 --> 01:03:04.000
so nobody who's watching should panic

01:03:04.000 --> 01:03:06.553
but as a thought experiment,

01:03:06.553 --> 01:03:09.550
break down the disciplinary
barriers completely,

01:03:09.550 --> 01:03:12.609
not have not have departments anymore,

01:03:12.609 --> 01:03:14.940
maybe not even have schools,

01:03:14.940 --> 01:03:18.630
admit people to PhD programs in science

01:03:18.630 --> 01:03:20.923
or humanities or social science.

01:03:24.560 --> 01:03:26.530
There's one university
out on the West Coast

01:03:26.530 --> 01:03:29.150
where faculty move between industry

01:03:29.150 --> 01:03:33.170
and the university with
almost no barriers.

01:03:33.170 --> 01:03:35.740
So one year there at Google,
one year they're at Stanford,

01:03:35.740 --> 01:03:37.260
doesn't seem to matter.

01:03:37.260 --> 01:03:39.590
And should we really go further?

01:03:39.590 --> 01:03:40.423
Should we?

01:03:41.720 --> 01:03:43.510
I think co-location is great.

01:03:43.510 --> 01:03:46.430
I think raising money is important

01:03:46.430 --> 01:03:48.730
and industrial collaboration

01:03:48.730 --> 01:03:51.870
has, I think, created new avenues.

01:03:51.870 --> 01:03:56.010
But should we, as Yale, or we as academia,

01:03:56.010 --> 01:03:58.467
however you want to answer it,

01:03:58.467 --> 01:04:01.720
should we go should we go even further?

01:04:01.720 --> 01:04:03.180
- Yeah, that's a great question, Peter,

01:04:03.180 --> 01:04:06.350
and it's one obviously
that I've gotten often.

01:04:06.350 --> 01:04:10.240
And it may surprise,
probably not you, but others

01:04:10.240 --> 01:04:13.470
that I actually, people would ask me,

01:04:13.470 --> 01:04:15.860
well, don't we need to change departments?

01:04:15.860 --> 01:04:18.770
And I wouldn't even know how to begin

01:04:18.770 --> 01:04:20.960
to think about changing departments.

01:04:20.960 --> 01:04:22.810
And people need an intellectual home

01:04:22.810 --> 01:04:25.389
and people need to know something, right?

01:04:25.389 --> 01:04:27.970
All of this is possible because the people

01:04:27.970 --> 01:04:29.760
who are participating are deeply rooted

01:04:29.760 --> 01:04:31.985
in a discipline and they know

01:04:31.985 --> 01:04:34.510
what that discipline brings them.

01:04:34.510 --> 01:04:37.700
And the question is how do
you provide the flexibility

01:04:37.700 --> 01:04:39.350
so that you can have the conversations

01:04:39.350 --> 01:04:41.480
with people from a different discipline

01:04:41.480 --> 01:04:43.100
to come up with a new idea?

01:04:43.100 --> 01:04:46.790
So, I often say, I don't
know what the names

01:04:46.790 --> 01:04:49.070
of huge departments would be

01:04:49.070 --> 01:04:51.130
but we need more flexibility.

01:04:51.130 --> 01:04:54.410
And I'll tell you, Peter,
you've probably seen this also.

01:04:54.410 --> 01:04:59.410
The students, the next generation
are lunatics about this.

01:05:00.140 --> 01:05:02.870
So they are, even with
the time I first arrived

01:05:02.870 --> 01:05:06.010
at MIT in 2004 and I would,
when the freshmen arrive,

01:05:06.010 --> 01:05:07.720
you do the same thing wander around campus

01:05:07.720 --> 01:05:08.920
and start up conversations

01:05:08.920 --> 01:05:11.780
with our new members of our community

01:05:11.780 --> 01:05:14.060
and I said, "What do you
think you might wanna do?"

01:05:14.060 --> 01:05:15.467
And a huge fraction said,

01:05:15.467 --> 01:05:18.970
"Oh, I wanna do biomedical
engineering," or I want to,

01:05:20.390 --> 01:05:23.020
and that has only increased.

01:05:23.020 --> 01:05:24.930
One of the things that we've done at MIT

01:05:24.930 --> 01:05:29.370
that I think is good for both
the faculty and the students

01:05:29.370 --> 01:05:30.890
is that when I arrived,

01:05:30.890 --> 01:05:34.660
the undergraduate majors
were disciplinary.

01:05:34.660 --> 01:05:37.890
And if you wanted to add
something on, you could

01:05:37.890 --> 01:05:40.560
but the students' schedules
are already pretty packed

01:05:40.560 --> 01:05:43.190
and it was doable but not easy.

01:05:43.190 --> 01:05:47.570
And now, we have a whole
array of hybrid majors.

01:05:47.570 --> 01:05:52.570
So you can major in biology
and computer science.

01:05:52.750 --> 01:05:57.120
You can major in your
biology and computer science.

01:05:57.120 --> 01:06:01.660
You can major in, there are a
number of these hybrid majors

01:06:01.660 --> 01:06:03.860
and it's important
because, for two reasons.

01:06:03.860 --> 01:06:06.021
One is the students wanna do it

01:06:06.021 --> 01:06:09.800
but the other reason it's
important is it gives our faculty

01:06:10.670 --> 01:06:13.600
a chance to pioneer these new fields

01:06:13.600 --> 01:06:18.600
because you know well that
it really is in architecting

01:06:19.870 --> 01:06:24.870
our majors or requirements
that we architect a field.

01:06:24.870 --> 01:06:26.360
That's the way we pioneer fields.

01:06:26.360 --> 01:06:29.100
And so, I think that I wouldn't abandon

01:06:29.100 --> 01:06:30.778
all of the organization
because people like,

01:06:30.778 --> 01:06:33.865
you're a psychologist,
you know better than I

01:06:33.865 --> 01:06:38.140
how much good comes out of
a more intimate community

01:06:38.140 --> 01:06:41.750
when that community is
situated in a candy store

01:06:41.750 --> 01:06:43.186
of other opportunities.

01:06:43.186 --> 01:06:48.010
So I'm not a fan of letting
everyone lose to self-associate

01:06:48.010 --> 01:06:51.430
but I am a fan of what I often describe

01:06:51.430 --> 01:06:55.360
as turning footpaths into super highways.

01:06:55.360 --> 01:06:57.370
You'll figure out how
to make the connection

01:06:57.370 --> 01:06:59.050
with a physicist do you wanna talk to

01:06:59.050 --> 01:07:00.790
for your experiments, right?

01:07:00.790 --> 01:07:03.150
And once you've kind
of bushwhacked your way

01:07:03.150 --> 01:07:04.670
between disciplines,

01:07:04.670 --> 01:07:06.390
why should the next
person have to go through

01:07:06.390 --> 01:07:08.300
that same bushwhacking process?

01:07:08.300 --> 01:07:10.873
And so putting in place the,

01:07:11.760 --> 01:07:13.460
as I said, the super highways

01:07:13.460 --> 01:07:15.411
rather than just the footpaths,

01:07:15.411 --> 01:07:17.530
I think is a way of creating this

01:07:17.530 --> 01:07:20.390
and I'm a really a huge believer,

01:07:20.390 --> 01:07:23.286
I have a lot of confidence
in bringing our students

01:07:23.286 --> 01:07:25.380
into this reinvention of the future

01:07:25.380 --> 01:07:28.464
by giving them the
opportunities to (chuckles),

01:07:28.464 --> 01:07:30.310
as I collect my...

01:07:30.310 --> 01:07:31.840
Be the plasmids.

01:07:31.840 --> 01:07:34.130
So the plasmids is are
these genetic elements

01:07:34.130 --> 01:07:36.713
that move knowledge from
one cell to another.

01:07:37.960 --> 01:07:41.170
And so the students really
are in a university,

01:07:41.170 --> 01:07:42.900
the information carrier carriers,

01:07:42.900 --> 01:07:44.500
that are the real people who are doing

01:07:44.500 --> 01:07:45.900
the bridging of disciplines.

01:07:47.240 --> 01:07:48.125
- Thank you, Susan.

01:07:48.125 --> 01:07:48.958
It's so good to see you.

01:07:48.958 --> 01:07:51.191
- [Susan] It's great to see you, Peter.

01:07:51.191 --> 01:07:52.970
- Thank you so much, Susan.

01:07:52.970 --> 01:07:56.308
And, I think, from your talk
we could see the imprint

01:07:56.308 --> 01:08:00.450
that you have left on the MIT campus.

01:08:00.450 --> 01:08:03.820
As an alum, I have seen the
transformation is incredible.

01:08:03.820 --> 01:08:06.580
I really hope you can come
to Yale, to our campus,

01:08:06.580 --> 01:08:08.294
and to see how things are changing

01:08:08.294 --> 01:08:11.360
in the geography and in terms

01:08:11.360 --> 01:08:12.670
of these intellectual connections.

01:08:12.670 --> 01:08:16.430
So we have a lot of questions
and I'm really tempted

01:08:16.430 --> 01:08:19.580
to ask you one myself before I launch.

01:08:19.580 --> 01:08:22.940
And that is you discussed
this a little bit in your book

01:08:22.940 --> 01:08:25.170
and I was curious about your views.

01:08:25.170 --> 01:08:29.330
So there's been talk
about for by economists

01:08:29.330 --> 01:08:31.640
like Mariana Mazzucato and others

01:08:31.640 --> 01:08:34.010
who've talked about how
maybe the federal government

01:08:34.010 --> 01:08:36.860
and federal funding
for blue skies research

01:08:36.860 --> 01:08:39.720
should have some incentivized way

01:08:39.720 --> 01:08:42.470
of returning some money
back to the government

01:08:42.470 --> 01:08:46.702
so that you can grow this blue skies fund

01:08:46.702 --> 01:08:49.150
from the federal side always.

01:08:49.150 --> 01:08:52.176
So for example, a simple
example that she often cites

01:08:52.176 --> 01:08:56.140
is Google that came out of
the NSS libraries project,

01:08:56.140 --> 01:09:01.140
if only they had said 0.001%,
if your idea gets monetized

01:09:01.960 --> 01:09:04.710
comes back for blue skies research.

01:09:04.710 --> 01:09:08.510
So I was very curious
whether what your views were

01:09:08.510 --> 01:09:13.510
on other than philanthropy
and industrial partnerships,

01:09:13.940 --> 01:09:17.100
whether you felt that
the federal government

01:09:17.100 --> 01:09:20.250
had a larger role to play in fostering

01:09:20.250 --> 01:09:22.890
this new kind of innovation.

01:09:22.890 --> 01:09:24.620
- Yeah, Priya, thanks for that question

01:09:24.620 --> 01:09:27.940
and you've touched on something
that I think is really key

01:09:27.940 --> 01:09:30.190
and most people don't appreciate,

01:09:30.190 --> 01:09:33.610
which is it is a nation that wants

01:09:33.610 --> 01:09:36.280
to be part of the industrial future

01:09:37.180 --> 01:09:42.180
has to invest in the
infrastructure, right?

01:09:42.460 --> 01:09:45.260
In the ground floor
because the ground floor,

01:09:45.260 --> 01:09:47.067
you don't know where it's
going, you have to invest

01:09:47.067 --> 01:09:48.950
and you have to do it wisely.

01:09:48.950 --> 01:09:51.280
Vannevar Bush was so brilliant

01:09:51.280 --> 01:09:54.690
in saying that we have to
provide federal funds for that

01:09:54.690 --> 01:09:56.930
because no one can afford to invest in it.

01:09:56.930 --> 01:10:00.140
You never make enough
money out it directly.

01:10:00.140 --> 01:10:02.620
But as you said, there's
a huge amount of money

01:10:02.620 --> 01:10:06.930
that's produced and we have to understand

01:10:06.930 --> 01:10:09.630
that investing federal funds

01:10:09.630 --> 01:10:13.150
in the first steps of this enterprise

01:10:13.150 --> 01:10:16.150
is absolutely required.

01:10:16.150 --> 01:10:17.930
The idea is you can buy
it from someone else.

01:10:17.930 --> 01:10:20.220
That's clearly a fool's errand.

01:10:20.220 --> 01:10:22.370
That has not worked
for any nation although

01:10:24.344 --> 01:10:26.391
we worry about patenting and copywriting.

01:10:26.391 --> 01:10:30.370
People often remind me that United States

01:10:30.370 --> 01:10:31.590
didn't have copyright laws

01:10:31.590 --> 01:10:33.130
until we started producing enough books

01:10:33.130 --> 01:10:35.620
and Britain was furious that, for example,

01:10:35.620 --> 01:10:37.880
Charles Dickens books were
published in the United States

01:10:37.880 --> 01:10:40.030
were never published in Britain

01:10:40.030 --> 01:10:44.570
because they were obviously,
done out outside of copyright.

01:10:44.570 --> 01:10:46.907
so I so I think it's
important for us to invest

01:10:46.907 --> 01:10:49.630
and I know, I mean obviously,

01:10:49.630 --> 01:10:51.850
I've been involved in you've been involved

01:10:51.850 --> 01:10:54.400
in telling the story of the critical role

01:10:54.400 --> 01:10:56.960
of blue skies research.

01:10:56.960 --> 01:11:01.270
But I do believe that, as you say,

01:11:01.270 --> 01:11:06.130
that the companies, the
industries that have profited

01:11:06.130 --> 01:11:08.780
from that research should be returning

01:11:08.780 --> 01:11:11.490
and frankly, they do return
some of those profits back

01:11:11.490 --> 01:11:14.170
into the into federal government.

01:11:14.170 --> 01:11:16.770
Federal government has to
decide how to use those funds

01:11:16.770 --> 01:11:18.790
and of course, there are infinite demands

01:11:18.790 --> 01:11:20.600
but I think it's absolutely essential

01:11:20.600 --> 01:11:25.270
and I just wish I had
kind of a magic formula

01:11:25.270 --> 01:11:28.120
to help people understand
how important it is.

01:11:28.120 --> 01:11:31.080
But I think that any nation

01:11:32.400 --> 01:11:37.070
that wants to be an
industrial and economic force

01:11:37.070 --> 01:11:42.070
has to invest as generously as imaginable

01:11:42.540 --> 01:11:43.410
in this kind of effort.

01:11:43.410 --> 01:11:44.980
That's why I showed these comparisons

01:11:44.980 --> 01:11:46.530
between the United States
and other countries.

01:11:46.530 --> 01:11:49.790
It's not a surprise that Germany
and South Korea and Japan

01:11:49.790 --> 01:11:52.360
industrial powerhouses are the countries

01:11:52.360 --> 01:11:53.750
that are investing more than we are

01:11:53.750 --> 01:11:55.933
as as a fraction of GDP, of course.

01:11:57.320 --> 01:11:58.450
- Great.

01:11:58.450 --> 01:12:03.320
Tamar, if you had a question,
now would be a good time to go

01:12:03.320 --> 01:12:04.863
because there's a real pileup.

01:12:05.890 --> 01:12:06.980
- There's a whole queue.

01:12:06.980 --> 01:12:09.440
I'm happy to pass my
spot on to a colleague.

01:12:09.440 --> 01:12:10.500
- Okay, great.

01:12:10.500 --> 01:12:13.890
So the first question
was from Justin Zaremby

01:12:13.890 --> 01:12:16.380
who said, "It's very
striking when Bell Labs

01:12:16.380 --> 01:12:18.350
got rid of its fundamental
physics research

01:12:18.350 --> 01:12:20.320
in about 2008 or so.

01:12:20.320 --> 01:12:22.840
What would it take for commercial ventures

01:12:22.840 --> 01:12:26.660
to explore basic research
alongside applied research

01:12:26.660 --> 01:12:29.150
or does the inefficiency of basic research

01:12:29.150 --> 01:12:33.140
limited to existing within
university settings solely?"

01:12:33.140 --> 01:12:33.973
- Hey, Justin.

01:12:33.973 --> 01:12:36.330
Thanks for thanks for joining us today.

01:12:36.330 --> 01:12:39.750
I was so happy to see your
name in the list of attendees.

01:12:39.750 --> 01:12:43.550
So, you've actually asked
a critical question.

01:12:43.550 --> 01:12:46.560
So why was there Bell Labs?

01:12:46.560 --> 01:12:49.083
Why was there a GE Research Lab?

01:12:50.040 --> 01:12:52.383
It was possible because those companies,

01:12:53.350 --> 01:12:55.350
I wouldn't go so far as
to call it a monopoly

01:12:55.350 --> 01:12:58.400
but they actually controlled
so much of the industry

01:12:58.400 --> 01:13:01.012
that they had the resources
and they were funding,

01:13:01.012 --> 01:13:03.800
frankly, not simply their own work

01:13:03.800 --> 01:13:06.893
but they had the resources
available to fund it and as,

01:13:08.413 --> 01:13:09.600
I'll use a kind word,

01:13:09.600 --> 01:13:13.533
as industrial economics
became more efficient,

01:13:14.390 --> 01:13:17.320
those kinds of resources
were not available

01:13:17.320 --> 01:13:18.510
or claim to be not available

01:13:18.510 --> 01:13:20.130
and so we should have very, very few

01:13:20.130 --> 01:13:21.392
industrial research labs.

01:13:21.392 --> 01:13:22.510
So the story of the Bell Labs

01:13:22.510 --> 01:13:26.357
was Bell Labs was, obviously, legendary

01:13:28.090 --> 01:13:29.470
for the kind of physics,

01:13:29.470 --> 01:13:32.360
fundamental physics research that they did

01:13:32.360 --> 01:13:37.260
and you might say this is
something that we could dream of

01:13:37.260 --> 01:13:40.200
but the economic structure
doesn't support it.

01:13:40.200 --> 01:13:42.400
And what would it require?

01:13:42.400 --> 01:13:46.300
It might require a kind
of federal accounting

01:13:46.300 --> 01:13:48.470
but how would you measure what research

01:13:48.470 --> 01:13:50.040
was really blue sky research

01:13:50.040 --> 01:13:53.480
and what research was
really the D side of R&amp;D,

01:13:53.480 --> 01:13:55.290
the development research?

01:13:55.290 --> 01:14:00.290
And I think it's my guess,
although I haven't been inside,

01:14:02.980 --> 01:14:07.650
the companies that are involved
in space exploration now,

01:14:07.650 --> 01:14:09.780
I don't know how much fundamental
research is going on there

01:14:09.780 --> 01:14:12.600
but my guess is probably not so much.

01:14:12.600 --> 01:14:16.100
So I actually, I'm a fan
of the system we have

01:14:16.100 --> 01:14:21.010
but I desperately wish that as a nation,

01:14:21.010 --> 01:14:25.333
we were as committed to our
future as we are to our present.

01:14:27.010 --> 01:14:28.260
- Great, thanks.

01:14:28.260 --> 01:14:31.550
The next question is
from James Fairweather

01:14:31.550 --> 01:14:33.700
and the question is do
you think that biology

01:14:33.700 --> 01:14:35.490
is quickly approaching the same level

01:14:35.490 --> 01:14:37.690
of development practice as software?

01:14:37.690 --> 01:14:39.937
So it's sort of the
idea of version control,

01:14:39.937 --> 01:14:43.690
software-defined vaccines,
patch upgrades of software

01:14:43.690 --> 01:14:45.940
to improve performance,
et cetera, et cetera.

01:14:45.940 --> 01:14:48.550
If so, how long you reckon before biology

01:14:48.550 --> 01:14:51.340
develops the same standards
of interoperability

01:14:51.340 --> 01:14:53.660
that accelerated mechanical engineering?

01:14:53.660 --> 01:14:56.010
Sort of the standardization, he's saying,

01:14:56.010 --> 01:14:58.520
of examples of thread sizes,
dimensioning, et cetera

01:14:58.520 --> 01:15:00.183
and in electronics with interfaces

01:15:00.183 --> 01:15:02.660
and discreet components and software

01:15:02.660 --> 01:15:05.550
with cloud software-defined
infrastructure.

01:15:05.550 --> 01:15:06.820
- It's a great question.

01:15:06.820 --> 01:15:09.540
So it's the industrialization
of the biology parts list

01:15:09.540 --> 01:15:10.593
and that's happening.

01:15:11.870 --> 01:15:16.870
It's not so hard to get a
biological piece that you need.

01:15:16.980 --> 01:15:21.250
I think one of the, and
I'm speaking about this

01:15:21.250 --> 01:15:23.670
as someone who knows very little

01:15:23.670 --> 01:15:26.323
about the world of computer science.

01:15:27.350 --> 01:15:31.930
One of the remarkable things about biology

01:15:31.930 --> 01:15:34.960
is we still are discovering new things.

01:15:34.960 --> 01:15:38.230
And so, it's not as
though we have completed

01:15:38.230 --> 01:15:39.590
the biology parts list.

01:15:39.590 --> 01:15:42.930
It's not as though we
understand all of the pieces

01:15:42.930 --> 01:15:45.410
that make a biological organism work.

01:15:45.410 --> 01:15:48.310
We are discovering new things every day.

01:15:48.310 --> 01:15:50.760
I mean I hesitate to bring
it to the conversation

01:15:50.760 --> 01:15:55.370
but CRISPR-Cas9, who would have thought

01:15:55.370 --> 01:15:57.000
that this kind of system existed?

01:15:57.000 --> 01:15:58.567
And not only have we discovered it

01:15:58.567 --> 01:16:00.790
but we also figured out how to deploy it

01:16:00.790 --> 01:16:02.530
for any number of uses.

01:16:02.530 --> 01:16:04.350
And so I don't know

01:16:04.350 --> 01:16:08.760
that this kind of
regularization standardization.

01:16:08.760 --> 01:16:11.120
Obviously, the elements themselves

01:16:12.740 --> 01:16:17.670
have a structure and a limitation to them.

01:16:17.670 --> 01:16:18.770
But I don't know whether we'll have

01:16:18.770 --> 01:16:21.989
the kind of standardization
that now exists

01:16:21.989 --> 01:16:25.930
or is evolving in the computer industry.

01:16:25.930 --> 01:16:26.763
Good question.

01:16:28.640 --> 01:16:32.290
- So the next question is from Barry Abel

01:16:32.290 --> 01:16:37.190
and he asks, all of this depends
on the brain as a resource,

01:16:37.190 --> 01:16:40.720
yet, the brain sciences
have somewhat lag behind,

01:16:40.720 --> 01:16:42.640
particularly psychiatry.

01:16:42.640 --> 01:16:45.610
Can you speak to the importance
of focusing this convergence

01:16:45.610 --> 01:16:48.403
on ways to help brains function better?

01:16:49.280 --> 01:16:50.350
- Yeah, it's a great question.

01:16:50.350 --> 01:16:53.340
As a neuroscientist, I feel quite humbled

01:16:53.340 --> 01:16:56.350
because neuroscience has moved more slowly

01:16:56.350 --> 01:16:59.630
than we would have dreamed
and it's another place

01:16:59.630 --> 01:17:02.533
where there was a lot yet to be known.

01:17:03.390 --> 01:17:08.310
And obviously, I've got a single tool

01:17:09.200 --> 01:17:10.370
to increase that knowledge

01:17:10.370 --> 01:17:13.763
and it's just increasing
the kind of research we do.

01:17:15.690 --> 01:17:19.720
And I think one of the
great disappointments

01:17:20.810 --> 01:17:24.630
of biology as it's evolved up to today

01:17:24.630 --> 01:17:29.630
is that we have yet to have
the kind of interventions

01:17:29.870 --> 01:17:33.460
that would allow for us to tackle

01:17:33.460 --> 01:17:36.820
the kinds of really horrible
diseases of the brain.

01:17:36.820 --> 01:17:39.880
There's a chapter in
the book about bionics

01:17:40.990 --> 01:17:44.810
and I have to say the progress

01:17:44.810 --> 01:17:49.020
in developing smart
prosthetics is breathtaking

01:17:49.020 --> 01:17:53.370
and the smartest prosthetics of today

01:17:53.370 --> 01:17:55.700
are worlds different

01:17:55.700 --> 01:17:59.210
from the dumbest prosthetics of yesterday.

01:17:59.210 --> 01:18:04.210
However, figuring out how to
regrow connections in the brain

01:18:05.870 --> 01:18:09.560
has been quite daunting
but I have confidence

01:18:09.560 --> 01:18:14.130
that with more of this kind
of fundamental research,

01:18:14.130 --> 01:18:15.610
we'll get someplace but I have to say,

01:18:15.610 --> 01:18:17.470
I've just broken the premise.

01:18:17.470 --> 01:18:19.500
So the premise is for blue sky researches,

01:18:19.500 --> 01:18:22.420
you don't know what you're gonna come upon

01:18:22.420 --> 01:18:25.800
and we desperately know what we need

01:18:25.800 --> 01:18:29.000
in terms of psychiatry and neurology.

01:18:29.000 --> 01:18:31.566
We're just having a hard time
putting the pieces together.

01:18:31.566 --> 01:18:33.550
And I would say as

01:18:36.910 --> 01:18:39.860
disappointing as that is,

01:18:39.860 --> 01:18:43.280
it also points to the fact

01:18:43.280 --> 01:18:45.560
that there's so much more
that we need to learn

01:18:45.560 --> 01:18:50.560
and so, it's a tough message to hear

01:18:50.830 --> 01:18:54.250
and as neuroscientists, we
hear that message every day.

01:18:54.250 --> 01:18:58.140
But I think that I can't
even begin to imagine

01:18:58.140 --> 01:18:59.720
where we're gonna be in five or 10 years

01:18:59.720 --> 01:19:03.050
but the progress has been incredibly rapid

01:19:03.050 --> 01:19:04.780
just in terms of how much we understand

01:19:04.780 --> 01:19:06.670
about the fundamental structure,

01:19:06.670 --> 01:19:08.283
the development of the brain.

01:19:11.430 --> 01:19:14.630
- Right, so while we sort out
the next set of questions,

01:19:14.630 --> 01:19:18.033
I have one burning question
so I'm gonna sneak it in here.

01:19:18.910 --> 01:19:22.845
So, Susan, a lot of what
you've discussed, right?

01:19:22.845 --> 01:19:26.680
We are talking about a
highly advanced country,

01:19:26.680 --> 01:19:30.663
these technologies that
might provide solutions

01:19:32.400 --> 01:19:34.890
primarily aimed at the wealthy.

01:19:34.890 --> 01:19:38.960
So, I was curious about how you thought

01:19:38.960 --> 01:19:42.610
we could move forward and defeat Malthus

01:19:42.610 --> 01:19:45.900
but also envision a more equitable world.

01:19:45.900 --> 01:19:46.970
Especially, right now,

01:19:46.970 --> 01:19:50.720
even with just the
COVID-19 vaccine rollout

01:19:50.720 --> 01:19:54.890
and this is a perfect
situation where the entire,

01:19:54.890 --> 01:19:57.920
for the world to be safe,
we all need to be vaccinated

01:19:57.920 --> 01:20:00.330
and it's not gonna be enough
just for the wealthy countries

01:20:00.330 --> 01:20:04.360
in the West to be vaccinated
given how global we are.

01:20:04.360 --> 01:20:08.320
So how do you see this convergence

01:20:08.320 --> 01:20:13.254
spreading and radiating out
in a more equitable way?

01:20:13.254 --> 01:20:15.990
Because our survival, we all know,

01:20:15.990 --> 01:20:19.640
is really bound up with each
other in such an intricate way.

01:20:19.640 --> 01:20:21.680
- Yeah, Priya, this is
such an important question

01:20:21.680 --> 01:20:26.340
and in the chapter on food and
agriculture, I do touch on it

01:20:26.340 --> 01:20:31.240
and one of the things
that is just stunning

01:20:33.000 --> 01:20:36.860
is the insufficiency, not
of advanced therapeutics,

01:20:36.860 --> 01:20:39.940
but frankly, just food and
water in the developing world

01:20:39.940 --> 01:20:41.920
and one of the problems
with water purification

01:20:41.920 --> 01:20:44.550
is this just gosh darn expensive.

01:20:44.550 --> 01:20:48.060
We're lousy at purifying
water and I offer an example

01:20:48.060 --> 01:20:50.610
of something that promises a
completely different approach

01:20:50.610 --> 01:20:51.860
to water purification

01:20:51.860 --> 01:20:55.130
that could be efficient
and could be economical.

01:20:55.130 --> 01:20:56.270
And the same thing with food.

01:20:56.270 --> 01:20:59.560
I mean, among, for me,
one of the tragedies

01:20:59.560 --> 01:21:04.560
of recent decades is
that there are viruses,

01:21:05.330 --> 01:21:07.640
there are plant viruses
that wipe out crops

01:21:07.640 --> 01:21:09.380
that the people rely on.

01:21:09.380 --> 01:21:14.380
Cassava is a crop that the
most needy people in the world

01:21:16.090 --> 01:21:19.330
rely to survive and cassava has a virus

01:21:19.330 --> 01:21:21.810
that will wipe out the entire cassava crop

01:21:21.810 --> 01:21:25.640
or leave people totally destitute

01:21:25.640 --> 01:21:30.640
and there is a way of
genetically modifying cassava,

01:21:31.340 --> 01:21:33.830
there's a strain of cassava
that has modified modified

01:21:33.830 --> 01:21:35.590
to resist the virus.

01:21:35.590 --> 01:21:40.590
And that crop was basically fought against

01:21:41.460 --> 01:21:43.670
because of people who are anti-GMO.

01:21:43.670 --> 01:21:47.670
And if you can people, why not help them?

01:21:47.670 --> 01:21:50.110
And so, it gets caught
up in a lot of politics

01:21:50.110 --> 01:21:51.306
and I think that does slow us down

01:21:51.306 --> 01:21:53.140
but I think there are so many people

01:21:53.140 --> 01:21:54.300
that are absolutely devoted

01:21:54.300 --> 01:21:57.276
to moving these kinds of
technologies and discoveries

01:21:57.276 --> 01:21:59.340
into the neediest population.

01:21:59.340 --> 01:22:01.060
If you look at what's happening

01:22:01.060 --> 01:22:03.250
with the vaccine distribution, we're slow.

01:22:03.250 --> 01:22:04.760
I mean I have to say vaccine distribution

01:22:04.760 --> 01:22:08.490
in Massachusetts has
been ridiculously poor

01:22:10.077 --> 01:22:14.560
but there are enough people, I think,

01:22:14.560 --> 01:22:18.430
and enough momentum to
actually get the vaccine out

01:22:18.430 --> 01:22:20.900
to people all over the world
who desperately need it.

01:22:20.900 --> 01:22:24.140
If you think about what we've
done with measles and smallpox

01:22:25.000 --> 01:22:28.960
in terms of distributing
these therapeutics

01:22:28.960 --> 01:22:33.460
and preventatives, I would
like there to be more

01:22:34.410 --> 01:22:36.840
but the awareness of it, I think,

01:22:36.840 --> 01:22:38.190
has increased dramatically.

01:22:40.330 --> 01:22:41.540
- Thank you so much.

01:22:41.540 --> 01:22:44.488
So I think, we are coming up
to 5:30 so I just wanted to end

01:22:44.488 --> 01:22:48.880
with one comment that Martin Coburn made

01:22:48.880 --> 01:22:50.760
which I thought was very interesting.

01:22:50.760 --> 01:22:52.880
He mentions that in 1969,

01:22:52.880 --> 01:22:56.070
the Nobel Laureate
physicist, Eugene Wigner

01:22:56.070 --> 01:22:58.480
gave a set of lectures at Yale

01:22:58.480 --> 01:23:01.490
and the last one was on the
future of quantum mechanics.

01:23:01.490 --> 01:23:03.810
And that his thesis was that the future

01:23:03.810 --> 01:23:05.400
was actually in biology.

01:23:05.400 --> 01:23:10.400
And Willis Lamb who was another
Nobel Laureate physicist

01:23:10.500 --> 01:23:12.870
took great exception to that idea,

01:23:12.870 --> 01:23:15.920
claiming that there were
no such applications.

01:23:15.920 --> 01:23:20.200
So, I guess the future really has shown us

01:23:20.200 --> 01:23:23.010
that there are these incredible synergies

01:23:23.010 --> 01:23:27.650
and convergences in
completely unexpected places.

01:23:27.650 --> 01:23:31.930
So thank you so much, Susan,
for this wonderful talk

01:23:31.930 --> 01:23:36.030
and for your vision
and for your leadership

01:23:36.030 --> 01:23:40.830
in executing many pieces by demonstration

01:23:40.830 --> 01:23:44.540
of how innovation in
the academic landscape

01:23:44.540 --> 01:23:46.010
ought to look like

01:23:46.010 --> 01:23:48.840
and how it could possibly
change all our lives

01:23:48.840 --> 01:23:49.900
for the better.

01:23:49.900 --> 01:23:52.590
So thank you so much for coming.

01:23:52.590 --> 01:23:56.400
We were hoping to have you come in person

01:23:56.400 --> 01:23:58.351
but we just didn't wanna wait any longer.

01:23:58.351 --> 01:24:02.160
we already postponed
it from last year and,

01:24:02.160 --> 01:24:05.580
but regardless of a talk,
you're always welcome, right?

01:24:05.580 --> 01:24:10.336
And Tamar, we would be
thrilled to have you on campus

01:24:10.336 --> 01:24:14.700
and come in person to New Haven.

01:24:14.700 --> 01:24:17.130
- Priya, Tamar, Peter, thank you so much

01:24:17.130 --> 01:24:18.690
for inviting me to be part of this.

01:24:18.690 --> 01:24:22.370
Even though virtual, I
can now feel the Yale love

01:24:22.370 --> 01:24:26.253
and I'm still part of you, guys.

01:24:27.180 --> 01:24:29.170
Looking forward to coming back on campus.

01:24:29.170 --> 01:24:31.380
- Yes, and I think, Peter and Tamar,

01:24:31.380 --> 01:24:33.290
we're planning up all kinds of parties

01:24:33.290 --> 01:24:34.670
and receptions and so on.

01:24:34.670 --> 01:24:39.380
So you have a rain check
for a visit to New Haven.

01:24:39.380 --> 01:24:41.950
Thank you very much,
everyone, for attending

01:24:41.950 --> 01:24:45.313
and I look forward to seeing
you at the next Franke event.

